1
00:00:01,301 --> 00:00:03,611
-♪ In the not-too-distant
future ♪

2
00:00:04,171 --> 00:00:07,412
♪ Next Sunday, AD

3
00:00:07,446 --> 00:00:10,309
♪ There was a guy named Joel

4
00:00:10,343 --> 00:00:13,653
♪ Not too different
from you or me ♪

5
00:00:13,688 --> 00:00:16,688
♪ He worked
at Gizmonic Institute ♪

6
00:00:16,722 --> 00:00:19,998
♪ Just another face
in a red jumpsuit ♪

7
00:00:20,033 --> 00:00:22,826
♪ He did a good job
cleaning up the place ♪

8
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:24,309
♪ But his bosses
didn't like him, ♪

9
00:00:24,343 --> 00:00:26,205
♪ so they shot him into space

10
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:29,240
♪♪

11
00:00:29,274 --> 00:00:32,309
♪ We'll send him
cheesy movies ♪

12
00:00:32,343 --> 00:00:34,412
♪ The worst we can find

13
00:00:34,446 --> 00:00:35,619
♪ La-la-la

14
00:00:35,653 --> 00:00:38,102
♪ He'll have to sit
and watch them all ♪

15
00:00:38,136 --> 00:00:40,102
♪ And we'll monitor his mind

16
00:00:40,136 --> 00:00:41,481
♪ La-la-la

17
00:00:41,515 --> 00:00:44,446
♪ Now, keep in mind,
Joel can't control ♪

18
00:00:44,481 --> 00:00:46,343
♪ Where the movies
begin or end ♪

19
00:00:46,378 --> 00:00:47,653
♪ La-la-la

20
00:00:47,688 --> 00:00:50,757
♪ Because he used
those special parts ♪

21
00:00:50,791 --> 00:00:55,240
♪ To make his robot friends

22
00:00:55,274 --> 00:00:57,067
♪ Robot roll call

23
00:00:57,102 --> 00:00:58,309
-Cambot!
-Pan left!

24
00:00:58,343 --> 00:00:59,860
-Gypsy!
-Hi, girl!

25
00:00:59,895 --> 00:01:01,412
-Tom Servo!
-What a cool guy!

26
00:01:01,446 --> 00:01:03,550
-Cro-o-o-ow!
-He's a wisecracker.

27
00:01:03,584 --> 00:01:06,171
-♪ If you're wondering
how he eats and breathes ♪

28
00:01:06,205 --> 00:01:09,240
♪ And other science facts --
la-la-la ♪

29
00:01:09,274 --> 00:01:11,722
♪ Then repeat to yourself,
It's just a show ♪

30
00:01:11,757 --> 00:01:14,998
♪ I should really just relax

31
00:01:15,033 --> 00:01:20,412
♪ For "Mystery Science
Theater 3000" ♪

32
00:01:20,446 --> 00:01:23,412
♪♪

33
00:01:23,446 --> 00:01:25,964
-♪ And the Blue
Ridge Mountains ♪

34
00:01:25,998 --> 00:01:27,515
-Clay!
Clay!

35
00:01:27,550 --> 00:01:29,550
I think I was spotted
on my way down here!

36
00:01:29,584 --> 00:01:31,998
-Did you wear your disguise?
-I was wearing my disguise,

37
00:01:32,033 --> 00:01:33,929
but I'm just not
very good in heels.

38
00:01:33,964 --> 00:01:36,653
-No one must know
we're down here doing this.

39
00:01:36,688 --> 00:01:38,136
-I'm sorry.

40
00:01:38,171 --> 00:01:40,722
-Well, it's time to call Joel
about the experiment.

41
00:01:40,757 --> 00:01:43,240
Come in,
Joely-Poely Puddin'-n-Pie!

42
00:01:43,274 --> 00:01:46,515
-Hey, sirs, I'm ready for
this week's invention exchange.

43
00:01:46,550 --> 00:01:48,550
Check this thing out.
I just made it.

44
00:01:48,584 --> 00:01:51,757
It's the world's only
electric bagpipes, all right?

45
00:01:51,791 --> 00:01:57,412
[Motor whirring,
bagpipes playing]

46
00:01:57,446 --> 00:02:01,964
♪ Amazing grace

47
00:02:01,998 --> 00:02:06,688
♪ How sweet the sound

48
00:02:06,722 --> 00:02:07,826
Okay.

49
00:02:07,860 --> 00:02:09,412
And, uh, the robots
and I have worked up

50
00:02:09,446 --> 00:02:10,757
a special cover version of

51
00:02:10,791 --> 00:02:12,067
Led Zeppelin's
"Whole Lotta Love."

52
00:02:12,102 --> 00:02:12,895
You ready, guys?

53
00:02:12,929 --> 00:02:13,895
-Ready!
-2, 3...

54
00:02:13,929 --> 00:02:15,102
-Rock it!

55
00:02:15,136 --> 00:02:17,860
-♪ She's gotta whole
lotta love ♪

56
00:02:17,895 --> 00:02:20,860
♪ Wanna whole lotta love

57
00:02:20,895 --> 00:02:24,033
♪ She's gotta whole
lotta love ♪

58
00:02:24,067 --> 00:02:25,929
♪ A really whole lotta love

59
00:02:28,205 --> 00:02:29,309
-I love it!

60
00:02:29,343 --> 00:02:31,446
Look, Larry's corneas
are bleeding.

61
00:02:31,481 --> 00:02:33,171
Oh.
Well!

62
00:02:33,205 --> 00:02:35,033
It's time we sent you
our experimental nugget

63
00:02:35,067 --> 00:02:36,412
this week, Joel.

64
00:02:36,446 --> 00:02:40,550
Now, human underarm perspirationis something that happens

65
00:02:40,584 --> 00:02:42,412
to everyone
after they go through puberty,

66
00:02:42,446 --> 00:02:46,378
which, I assume,
includes you, Joel.

67
00:02:46,412 --> 00:02:48,274
When's the last time
you saw a dog sweat?

68
00:02:48,309 --> 00:02:49,446
Larry?
-Never!

69
00:02:49,481 --> 00:02:51,171
-Exactly!
And why is that?

70
00:02:51,205 --> 00:02:52,584
-Dogs don't sweat!
That's why!

71
00:02:52,619 --> 00:02:55,688
-Exactly! Because of the dog's
pineal gland.

72
00:02:55,722 --> 00:02:58,309
Nature's own
canine antiperspirant.

73
00:02:58,343 --> 00:03:01,309
Now, you take the pineal gland,
and you make a serum.

74
00:03:01,343 --> 00:03:03,584
You get a dog -- it doesn't
matter what kind of dog --

75
00:03:03,619 --> 00:03:06,998
and you inject that serum
into a human subject --

76
00:03:07,033 --> 00:03:10,584
in this case, Larry.

77
00:03:10,619 --> 00:03:11,895
Now, let's see.

78
00:03:11,929 --> 00:03:13,757
It's so hard to find a spot
I haven't hit.

79
00:03:13,791 --> 00:03:16,343
Uh, what's this flower?

80
00:03:16,378 --> 00:03:17,895
And who's Roseanne?

81
00:03:17,929 --> 00:03:19,515
-Oh, just stick it, will you?

82
00:03:19,550 --> 00:03:21,550
-Sail on, Silver Bird!

83
00:03:21,584 --> 00:03:22,895
-D'oh!
Geez!

84
00:03:22,929 --> 00:03:26,102
-Now, instantly, the serum racesthrough the bloodstream

85
00:03:26,136 --> 00:03:28,136
like a Porsche Targa 911,

86
00:03:28,171 --> 00:03:31,205
commandeering each pore,
slamming it shut,

87
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:34,481
like the vault at your favorite
savings and loan!

88
00:03:34,515 --> 00:03:37,136
And, checking
the wetness sensors,

89
00:03:37,171 --> 00:03:41,343
we see that they are free
from wetness and/or odor.

90
00:03:44,964 --> 00:03:46,550
-Antidote.
-Oh! Yes, the antidote.

91
00:03:46,584 --> 00:03:48,309
-Antidote.
There you go.

92
00:03:48,343 --> 00:03:49,757
The antidote.

93
00:03:49,791 --> 00:03:51,998
And here is your treat.

94
00:03:54,033 --> 00:03:56,791
-Oh, brother.
-That was pathetic!

95
00:03:56,826 --> 00:03:58,446
-Eww!
-Hey, no, I thought

96
00:03:58,481 --> 00:04:00,136
that was really good, you guys.

97
00:04:00,171 --> 00:04:02,826
You're doing really well,
and I think that someday,

98
00:04:02,860 --> 00:04:04,998
you'll be ready
for the Nobel Prize.

99
00:04:05,033 --> 00:04:07,446
-Maybe for fiction!
-Hey!

100
00:04:07,481 --> 00:04:08,826
Hey, I noticed you moved.

101
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:11,515
You guys must've got kicked out
of Gizmonic Institute

102
00:04:11,550 --> 00:04:14,343
for shooting us
into space like this, I bet.

103
00:04:14,378 --> 00:04:17,550
-Oh, don't be ridiculous!
We moved!

104
00:04:17,584 --> 00:04:19,136
-It's our grand re-opening!

105
00:04:19,171 --> 00:04:21,964
Uh, welcome to Deep 13!

106
00:04:21,998 --> 00:04:23,481
-Deep 13?
Wait a minute!

107
00:04:23,515 --> 00:04:26,136
That's in the sub-basement
of Gizmonic Institute!

108
00:04:26,171 --> 00:04:28,722
I had to clean up
a flubber spill once there.

109
00:04:28,757 --> 00:04:30,826
It's incredibly radioactive!

110
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:33,309
-But it hasn't affected
our brain any.

111
00:04:33,343 --> 00:04:34,515
-We like it here!

112
00:04:34,550 --> 00:04:37,619
Now we're even closer
to the atomic pile.

113
00:04:37,653 --> 00:04:39,515
And one day...

114
00:04:39,550 --> 00:04:41,653
-Well, I suppose it's time
for you guys to start

115
00:04:41,688 --> 00:04:43,412
experimenting on us again.

116
00:04:43,446 --> 00:04:44,964
-I'll tell you when it's time
to do the movie,

117
00:04:44,998 --> 00:04:47,067
you squinty-eyed space chimp!

118
00:04:47,102 --> 00:04:49,240
-Oh, uh, Clay?
-What?

119
00:04:49,274 --> 00:04:51,240
-It is time.
-Oh.

120
00:04:51,274 --> 00:04:52,584
Yeah, I --
-Nice insult, though.

121
00:04:52,619 --> 00:04:54,412
-I knew that.
Thank you.

122
00:04:54,446 --> 00:04:56,826
Well, it's a real stinkburger
of a film this week, Joel.

123
00:04:56,860 --> 00:04:59,136
It's called "The Crawling Eye."

124
00:04:59,171 --> 00:05:02,929
-Oh, it's got a bad audio track,it's in black-and-white,

125
00:05:02,964 --> 00:05:05,860
and worst of all,
it stars Forrest Tucker.

126
00:05:05,895 --> 00:05:08,929
-Hmm.
Good name, bad actor.

127
00:05:08,964 --> 00:05:10,515
I'll put in the tape.

128
00:05:10,550 --> 00:05:14,860
-[Laughing maniacally]

129
00:05:14,895 --> 00:05:17,205
-Movie sign!

130
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:19,688
[Motors whirring]

131
00:05:27,343 --> 00:05:30,309
[Wind howling]

132
00:05:35,722 --> 00:05:37,895
-This must be
a Paramount picture.

133
00:05:37,929 --> 00:05:41,033
Pardon me.
-Yep.

134
00:05:41,067 --> 00:05:43,481
-Beautiful mountain.

135
00:05:43,515 --> 00:05:46,791
-Hey, Jimmy!
-Hang on a minute!

136
00:05:46,826 --> 00:05:49,274
Come see!

137
00:05:49,309 --> 00:05:52,412
-Hey, Jimmy!
-Quit -- quit shouting!

138
00:05:52,446 --> 00:05:53,619
-...you say?

139
00:05:53,653 --> 00:05:55,446
-I tell you, it's foggy.

140
00:05:55,481 --> 00:05:56,826
-It's a mountain.

141
00:05:56,860 --> 00:05:58,584
It's supposed to be cold.

142
00:05:58,619 --> 00:06:01,343
-That's bad Cary Grant
up there, isn't it?

143
00:06:01,378 --> 00:06:04,722
-Who is it, Jim?
The abominable snowman?

144
00:06:04,757 --> 00:06:06,895
-I tell you,
there's someone coming.

145
00:06:06,929 --> 00:06:09,171
[Beeping]
-Funny.

146
00:06:09,205 --> 00:06:11,412
-Oh, very good one.
-Who is it?

147
00:06:11,446 --> 00:06:13,584
-Look!
No!

148
00:06:13,619 --> 00:06:16,722
No-o-o-o!
-Going down.

149
00:06:16,757 --> 00:06:17,998
-Gerald!

150
00:06:18,033 --> 00:06:19,584
Help!

151
00:06:19,619 --> 00:06:23,412
-Hey, let me help you there.

152
00:06:23,446 --> 00:06:25,653
-Mountain flossing really
"rappels" me.

153
00:06:25,688 --> 00:06:27,998
-Now pick it up.
-Heave!

154
00:06:28,033 --> 00:06:29,860
Ho!

155
00:06:29,895 --> 00:06:31,688
Heave!

156
00:06:31,722 --> 00:06:33,309
-Can you see him?

157
00:06:33,343 --> 00:06:36,171
-Not yet!
He's just below the lip.

158
00:06:36,205 --> 00:06:39,550
-Oh, he got that hat
from Mike Nesmith.

159
00:06:39,584 --> 00:06:40,653
-Here he is!

160
00:06:40,688 --> 00:06:42,550
Jim!

161
00:06:42,584 --> 00:06:44,412
No!

162
00:06:44,446 --> 00:06:46,446
-Come on! Come on!

163
00:06:55,171 --> 00:06:56,481
You idiot!
We nearly had him.

164
00:06:56,515 --> 00:06:57,515
Why did you let him go?!

165
00:06:57,550 --> 00:06:58,584
-Didn't you see him?!

166
00:06:58,619 --> 00:07:01,067
-What are you talking about?
-His head!

167
00:07:01,102 --> 00:07:02,722
It was torn off!

168
00:07:02,757 --> 00:07:04,722
-You say that like
it's a bad thing.

169
00:07:04,757 --> 00:07:10,343
[Eerie music playing]
-"The Crawling Eye."

170
00:07:10,378 --> 00:07:11,584
Starring...

171
00:07:11,619 --> 00:07:13,757
-Should be "The Crawling Knee."

172
00:07:13,791 --> 00:07:16,171
-Oh, Forrest Tucker.

173
00:07:16,205 --> 00:07:18,515
He's from "F Troop."

174
00:07:18,550 --> 00:07:21,550
-Hey, Joel, how you supposed
to read these titles?

175
00:07:21,584 --> 00:07:23,136
-Oh, well, Crow, it's simple.

176
00:07:23,171 --> 00:07:24,929
You see the line up there?

177
00:07:24,964 --> 00:07:26,136
-Uh-huh.
-That's -- See?

178
00:07:26,171 --> 00:07:28,033
There's one there.
-Oh.

179
00:07:28,067 --> 00:07:30,757
-Follow the line, and it shows
you right what to look at.

180
00:07:30,791 --> 00:07:32,515
-"We are here."
-Yeah.

181
00:07:35,722 --> 00:07:39,653
They quit doing it 'cause
people started figuring it out.

182
00:07:39,688 --> 00:07:43,688
♪♪

183
00:07:43,722 --> 00:07:45,860
-Kind of like titles
with idiot clips.

184
00:07:45,895 --> 00:07:47,136
-Yeah.

185
00:07:47,171 --> 00:07:50,619
♪♪

186
00:07:50,653 --> 00:07:52,205
-Whoa.

187
00:07:52,240 --> 00:07:57,584
♪♪

188
00:07:57,619 --> 00:07:58,860
-Duncan Sutherland.

189
00:07:58,895 --> 00:08:01,102
Duncan.
What a yo-yo.

190
00:08:01,136 --> 00:08:03,550
-Good one, Tom.
-Thank you so much.

191
00:08:08,757 --> 00:08:10,757
-They're the men responsible.

192
00:08:10,791 --> 00:08:15,343
♪♪

193
00:08:15,378 --> 00:08:17,171
-Directed by us!

194
00:08:17,205 --> 00:08:23,653
♪♪

195
00:08:23,688 --> 00:08:24,895
-Tempean.

196
00:08:24,929 --> 00:08:27,964
-The light at the end
of the credits.

197
00:08:27,998 --> 00:08:29,274
-Oh!

198
00:08:29,309 --> 00:08:33,171
[Locomotive chugging]

199
00:08:33,205 --> 00:08:35,274
-Let's see, Gemini, Gemini --

200
00:08:35,309 --> 00:08:36,860
"You'll be attracted
to a crawling eye.

201
00:08:36,895 --> 00:08:38,619
Leos figure prominently."
Hmm.

202
00:08:38,653 --> 00:08:40,102
-Anne?

203
00:08:40,136 --> 00:08:42,343
-Mm.
-Were you dreaming?

204
00:08:44,964 --> 00:08:46,791
-Oh, I don't think so.

205
00:08:46,826 --> 00:08:48,171
Why?

206
00:08:48,205 --> 00:08:49,722
-You were talking in your sleep.

207
00:08:49,757 --> 00:08:52,860
-I give away any secrets?

208
00:08:52,895 --> 00:08:54,929
-Just that you're a man.
-Not really.

209
00:08:54,964 --> 00:08:57,205
I didn't quite catch
his last name.

210
00:08:57,240 --> 00:08:59,446
[Chuckles]

211
00:08:59,481 --> 00:09:01,136
Would you like some tea?

212
00:09:01,171 --> 00:09:03,860
-No, thank you.

213
00:09:03,895 --> 00:09:05,929
-You sure you feel all right?
-What a babe.

214
00:09:05,964 --> 00:09:07,343
-I feel wonderful.

215
00:09:07,378 --> 00:09:09,757
I wish everybody would stop
treating me like an invalid.

216
00:09:09,791 --> 00:09:13,343
-How about a wheelchair?

217
00:09:13,378 --> 00:09:15,412
-Look, Anne,
there are the mountains.

218
00:09:15,446 --> 00:09:17,102
-Hmm.

219
00:09:19,895 --> 00:09:22,481
[Ominous music plays]

220
00:09:22,515 --> 00:09:26,826
-"I am Mount Svengali.

221
00:09:26,860 --> 00:09:30,102
You will do as I say."

222
00:09:30,136 --> 00:09:34,343
♪♪

223
00:09:34,378 --> 00:09:36,274
Whoa.
"Hey, my paper!

224
00:09:36,309 --> 00:09:37,412
What the --"
-"Hey!

225
00:09:37,446 --> 00:09:39,860
Dibs, dibs!
Everything on my lap I get!"

226
00:09:42,929 --> 00:09:44,412
-What happened?
Did I -- Did I faint?

227
00:09:44,446 --> 00:09:46,412
-Now, don't worry,
it's all right.

228
00:09:46,446 --> 00:09:48,067
-Is there anything I can do?

229
00:09:48,102 --> 00:09:50,998
-You're Sergeant O'Rourke
from "F Troop."

230
00:09:51,033 --> 00:09:54,860
-Oh, uh, m-my name is
Alan Brooks.

231
00:09:54,895 --> 00:09:58,136
-Oh.
Well, I'm Anne Pilgrim.

232
00:09:58,171 --> 00:09:59,895
And this is my sister, Sarah.

233
00:09:59,929 --> 00:10:01,136
-Hello.
-Billy.

234
00:10:01,171 --> 00:10:02,378
-How do you do?

235
00:10:02,412 --> 00:10:04,584
Uh...

236
00:10:04,619 --> 00:10:06,274
There we are.

237
00:10:08,653 --> 00:10:10,067
-That paper's shot.

238
00:10:10,102 --> 00:10:12,446
-I'm sorry about your newspaper,
Mr. Brooks.

239
00:10:12,481 --> 00:10:15,378
-Oh, that's all right.
I read the baseball scores.

240
00:10:15,412 --> 00:10:16,550
There you are.
-It's just a prop, anyway.

241
00:10:16,584 --> 00:10:17,998
-Shot of that'll bring
the color back.

242
00:10:18,033 --> 00:10:19,895
-It's red dye number 7.

243
00:10:22,929 --> 00:10:24,205
-Looks like you might do
with one.

244
00:10:24,240 --> 00:10:25,826
-No, thanks.

245
00:10:25,860 --> 00:10:27,964
-You have far to go?

246
00:10:27,998 --> 00:10:29,205
-Geneva.

247
00:10:29,240 --> 00:10:32,378
-Oh, it's a big convention town,
isn't it?

248
00:10:32,412 --> 00:10:35,653
-Next, Trollenberg.
-Trollenberg?

249
00:10:35,688 --> 00:10:37,688
-Yes, Trollenberg.

250
00:10:37,722 --> 00:10:38,929
-Sarah, we're getting off
at Trollenberg.

251
00:10:38,964 --> 00:10:40,791
-That's my stop.

252
00:10:40,826 --> 00:10:41,929
-What's the matter, Anne?

253
00:10:41,964 --> 00:10:43,136
You know we have
to go on to Geneva --

254
00:10:43,171 --> 00:10:45,550
-No, I really can't go
any further today.

255
00:10:45,584 --> 00:10:47,584
-We can stop
at the Hotel Europa.

256
00:10:47,619 --> 00:10:48,895
-Hotel Europa?

257
00:10:48,929 --> 00:10:50,102
We've never been to this place.

258
00:10:50,136 --> 00:10:52,136
How do you know
about the Hotel Europa?

259
00:10:55,688 --> 00:10:58,343
-I don't know.

260
00:10:58,378 --> 00:11:01,446
-Anne, what's got into you?

261
00:11:01,481 --> 00:11:02,998
-We're getting off
at Trollenberg.

262
00:11:03,033 --> 00:11:05,412
Please, Sarah!

263
00:11:05,446 --> 00:11:07,136
-"Don't argue with me."

264
00:11:11,102 --> 00:11:13,033
"Trollenberg, home
of the crawling eye!

265
00:11:13,067 --> 00:11:15,067
All stops lead
to a bloody death!"

266
00:11:21,757 --> 00:11:23,343
-It's Mr. Haney!

267
00:11:23,378 --> 00:11:25,343
-Oh, Herr Brooks.
Sorry I'm late.

268
00:11:25,378 --> 00:11:26,860
Did you have a nice trip,
Herr Brooks?

269
00:11:26,895 --> 00:11:28,446
-Herr Klein,
nice to see you again.

270
00:11:28,481 --> 00:11:31,240
Oh, I'd like you to meet
Miss Anne and Sarah Pilgrim.

271
00:11:31,274 --> 00:11:32,791
-"They'll drink anything."
-...the proprietor of

272
00:11:32,826 --> 00:11:34,102
the Hotel Europa.

273
00:11:34,136 --> 00:11:35,515
Wonder if maybe
you could help us out.

274
00:11:35,550 --> 00:11:38,136
These young ladies have arrived
without any reservations,

275
00:11:38,171 --> 00:11:39,584
but perhaps
you could put them up.

276
00:11:39,619 --> 00:11:42,033
-Yes, uh...
Yes, it would be all right.

277
00:11:42,067 --> 00:11:43,309
-Good.
-Thank you very much.

278
00:11:43,343 --> 00:11:46,240
-The car's outside.

279
00:11:46,274 --> 00:11:48,309
-Very good of you to put us up
at such short notice.

280
00:11:48,343 --> 00:11:51,584
-Well, uh, this is not yet
our season, you understand?

281
00:11:51,619 --> 00:11:53,688
-Oh, so now would've been
about your busiest time?

282
00:11:53,722 --> 00:11:55,791
-Well, normally, yes, but, uh...

283
00:11:55,826 --> 00:11:57,826
-"We've been having trouble
with the crawling eye."

284
00:11:57,860 --> 00:11:59,102
-Is Hans still working for you?

285
00:11:59,136 --> 00:12:01,309
-Yes, sir, Hans is still
working for us, Herr Brooks.

286
00:12:01,343 --> 00:12:02,964
Thank you.

287
00:12:06,274 --> 00:12:08,274
-Cigarette, Sarah?
-No, thank you.

288
00:12:08,309 --> 00:12:12,378
-Hey, look, you guys -- they're
being followed by a movie.

289
00:12:12,412 --> 00:12:13,791
-No, thank you.

290
00:12:13,826 --> 00:12:15,412
-How about you, Herr Klein?
-Thank you, I don't smoke.

291
00:12:15,446 --> 00:12:18,378
-They shouldn't have
started to climb.

292
00:12:18,412 --> 00:12:19,688
-What are you talking about?

293
00:12:19,722 --> 00:12:21,274
-An accident.

294
00:12:21,309 --> 00:12:24,757
Last week, 1,200 feet
up the south col.

295
00:12:24,791 --> 00:12:28,826
Three English students --
one of them was killed.

296
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:31,550
-Was there an accident,
Mr. Klein?

297
00:12:31,584 --> 00:12:33,067
-On a mountain, uh...

298
00:12:33,102 --> 00:12:35,929
These things sometimes happen.

299
00:12:35,964 --> 00:12:38,688
-What else do you know
about the Trollenberg?

300
00:12:38,722 --> 00:12:40,929
-Peasants are leaving
the mountain.

301
00:12:40,964 --> 00:12:43,446
They say it's bad luck.

302
00:12:43,481 --> 00:12:46,515
-The mountain people
are very simple.

303
00:12:46,550 --> 00:12:48,309
They are superstitious.

304
00:12:48,343 --> 00:12:50,067
Oh...

305
00:12:50,102 --> 00:12:52,515
All these stories are nonsense.

306
00:12:52,550 --> 00:12:55,205
-What stories?
-Nonsense stories.

307
00:12:55,240 --> 00:12:59,998
-Disappearing into the mist
and never seen again.

308
00:13:00,033 --> 00:13:02,274
-She isn't foreign.

309
00:13:02,309 --> 00:13:05,067
-When in Switzerland,
visit Trollenberg.

310
00:13:07,998 --> 00:13:09,860
-It's really picturesque.

311
00:13:09,895 --> 00:13:17,515
♪♪

312
00:13:17,550 --> 00:13:23,653
[Ominous music playing]

313
00:13:23,688 --> 00:13:26,171
What a lovely matte painting.

314
00:13:26,205 --> 00:13:29,412
♪♪

315
00:13:29,446 --> 00:13:31,964
-Hans!
[Speaking German]

316
00:13:31,998 --> 00:13:35,171
-[Imitating German]
"What's that supposed to mean?"

317
00:13:35,205 --> 00:13:38,274
-Means, "The babes are coming."
-Oh.

318
00:13:38,309 --> 00:13:42,481
-There they are.
-Ooh.

319
00:13:42,515 --> 00:13:44,860
-Hello.
You must be Mr. Brooks.

320
00:13:44,895 --> 00:13:46,446
Klein told us you were coming.

321
00:13:46,481 --> 00:13:48,136
My name's Truscott,
Philip Truscott.

322
00:13:48,171 --> 00:13:49,584
-How are you, Mr Truscott?

323
00:13:49,619 --> 00:13:51,929
-Klein, you didn't tell us
we were expecting other guests.

324
00:13:51,964 --> 00:13:54,550
-Oh, this is Miss Sarah Pilgrim
and Miss Anne Pilgrim.

325
00:13:54,584 --> 00:13:56,481
-How do you do?
-"They'll drink anything."

326
00:13:56,515 --> 00:13:58,515
-Sarah and Anne Pilgrim.

327
00:13:58,550 --> 00:14:00,860
Swear we've met before.

328
00:14:00,895 --> 00:14:02,136
The name rings a bell.

329
00:14:02,171 --> 00:14:03,619
-Never been.

330
00:14:03,653 --> 00:14:05,998
Would you mind if we went up
to our rooms now?

331
00:14:08,240 --> 00:14:11,515
-"These aren't my clothes.

332
00:14:11,550 --> 00:14:13,412
I don't look good
in stripes at all."

333
00:14:13,446 --> 00:14:17,446
[Ominous music playing]

334
00:14:17,481 --> 00:14:19,067
-She can eat corn on the cob
through a picket fence.

335
00:14:19,102 --> 00:14:21,688
-It's all there.

336
00:14:21,722 --> 00:14:24,653
The observatory at the foot
of the mountain.

337
00:14:24,688 --> 00:14:27,240
The cable car.

338
00:14:27,274 --> 00:14:28,757
There's a small hut
the climbers use.

339
00:14:28,791 --> 00:14:32,309
You can see it
from the observatory.

340
00:14:32,343 --> 00:14:36,274
It's all there, just like...

341
00:14:36,309 --> 00:14:38,895
-Just like what, Anne?

342
00:14:38,929 --> 00:14:40,102
-Well, just like...

343
00:14:40,136 --> 00:14:44,378
♪♪

344
00:14:44,412 --> 00:14:46,481
Sarah, why did I want
to come here?

345
00:14:46,515 --> 00:14:47,929
What is there about this place?

346
00:14:47,964 --> 00:14:50,067
Why do I feel
I've seen it all before?

347
00:14:50,102 --> 00:14:51,826
-Don't worry about it, darling.

348
00:14:51,860 --> 00:14:53,895
Lots of people
get a feeling like that.

349
00:14:53,929 --> 00:14:55,791
Perhaps you read about it
in a book

350
00:14:55,826 --> 00:14:57,412
or saw a picture
in a travel photo.

351
00:14:57,446 --> 00:14:59,136
You mustn't let it affect you.

352
00:15:01,274 --> 00:15:03,860
-No, I mustn't.

353
00:15:03,895 --> 00:15:05,446
Yes, you're right.

354
00:15:05,481 --> 00:15:06,895
I've probably read about it.

355
00:15:06,929 --> 00:15:08,515
-In the script.

356
00:15:08,550 --> 00:15:11,515
♪♪

357
00:15:11,550 --> 00:15:16,240
[Theme music playing]

358
00:15:20,895 --> 00:15:22,550
-Hi.
-How are you?

359
00:15:22,584 --> 00:15:24,929
-Give you a hand?

360
00:15:24,964 --> 00:15:26,584
-Well, I think I can manage.

361
00:15:29,412 --> 00:15:31,033
-Lovely spot, this, isn't it?

362
00:15:31,067 --> 00:15:32,274
-Certainly is.

363
00:15:32,309 --> 00:15:33,653
-You've been here before,
haven't you?

364
00:15:33,688 --> 00:15:36,446
-Yes, once, a couple years ago.

365
00:15:36,481 --> 00:15:39,309
-Good climbing.
-So they tell me.

366
00:15:39,343 --> 00:15:42,136
-Don't you climb?
-Not if I can help it.

367
00:15:42,171 --> 00:15:43,136
-Must be his overnight gun.

368
00:15:43,171 --> 00:15:45,757
-Odd place to come
for holiday...

369
00:15:45,791 --> 00:15:48,964
if you don't climb.

370
00:15:48,998 --> 00:15:52,309
-Hmm. Yes, isn't it.

371
00:15:52,343 --> 00:15:54,860
-"Oh, this old thing?
Saved my life once."

372
00:15:54,895 --> 00:15:57,481
-Taxing,
those two girls, aren't they?

373
00:15:57,515 --> 00:16:00,791
-Certainly are.
-The Pilgrim sisters.

374
00:16:00,826 --> 00:16:03,550
Sarah and Anne...

375
00:16:03,584 --> 00:16:06,446
Of course I knew I'd seen them
before somewhere.

376
00:16:06,481 --> 00:16:08,136
On the Palladium,
about a month ago.

377
00:16:08,171 --> 00:16:09,791
They're a mind-reading act.

378
00:16:09,826 --> 00:16:10,929
-Hmm.

379
00:16:10,964 --> 00:16:14,688
-Funny they didn't say anything.

380
00:16:14,722 --> 00:16:17,964
-Well, perhaps they, uh --
they wanted some privacy.

381
00:16:17,998 --> 00:16:20,309
-"Just like me."

382
00:16:20,343 --> 00:16:21,860
-And I'll buy you a drink later.

383
00:16:21,895 --> 00:16:23,929
-All right.

384
00:16:23,964 --> 00:16:26,412
-Good.
-He knows how to take a hint.

385
00:16:29,619 --> 00:16:32,722
-He's kind of testy.

386
00:16:32,757 --> 00:16:35,826
-Give me Zurich-6468, will you?

387
00:16:35,860 --> 00:16:36,895
-"All right."

388
00:16:43,240 --> 00:16:46,481
-Yes, I want you to check
on someone for me.

389
00:16:46,515 --> 00:16:47,826
-Alan Brooks.
-"Alan Brooks?

390
00:16:47,860 --> 00:16:49,067
Okay.

391
00:16:49,102 --> 00:16:50,412
I'm checking on it."

392
00:16:50,446 --> 00:16:53,378
-He's American.
He's about 40, I should think.

393
00:16:53,412 --> 00:16:54,446
-"Was he on F Troop?"

394
00:16:54,481 --> 00:16:55,791
-Why, you should
try New York first

395
00:16:55,826 --> 00:16:57,757
and then Los Angeles
and then Washington.

396
00:16:57,791 --> 00:16:59,860
-"All right."
-Okay.

397
00:16:59,895 --> 00:17:01,102
-"Gotta go now."

398
00:17:01,136 --> 00:17:02,757
-Right.

399
00:17:11,205 --> 00:17:13,274
-Hello, there.
You'll be Brooks.

400
00:17:13,309 --> 00:17:14,481
-"If you want me to."

401
00:17:14,515 --> 00:17:16,171
-I'm Dewhurst.
This is Brett.

402
00:17:16,205 --> 00:17:17,826
-"We're the Hat Brothers."
-How do you do?

403
00:17:17,860 --> 00:17:20,205
-Looks like you're going
for a climb.

404
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:22,033
-Yes, we're going up
the Trollenberg.

405
00:17:22,067 --> 00:17:23,481
Gonna have a noggin
before we start.

406
00:17:23,515 --> 00:17:25,067
Care to join us?

407
00:17:25,102 --> 00:17:27,067
-All right.
-What's it going to be?

408
00:17:27,102 --> 00:17:28,309
-Scotch, please.

409
00:17:28,343 --> 00:17:30,446
-Scotch and two brandies.

410
00:17:30,481 --> 00:17:32,895
-Nothing like a little drinking
and climbing.

411
00:17:32,929 --> 00:17:34,929
-Keep the cold out tonight.
-You, uh --

412
00:17:34,964 --> 00:17:36,757
You're gonna stay the night?

413
00:17:36,791 --> 00:17:38,584
-No, there's a hut at the foot
of the south col.

414
00:17:38,619 --> 00:17:39,895
We'll sleep there
tonight and attack

415
00:17:39,929 --> 00:17:41,412
the mountain proper tomorrow.

416
00:17:41,446 --> 00:17:43,378
-"Unless it attacks us first."

417
00:17:43,412 --> 00:17:45,033
-Hello, Truscott.

418
00:17:45,067 --> 00:17:47,412
-"Who's the tomato?"
-You must be Miss Pilgrim?

419
00:17:47,446 --> 00:17:48,412
-Yes, that's right.

420
00:17:48,446 --> 00:17:50,584
-Oh, uh,
this is Mr. Dewhurst, Sarah.

421
00:17:50,619 --> 00:17:51,653
And Mister...

422
00:17:51,688 --> 00:17:53,274
-Brett.
-Brett, yes.

423
00:17:53,309 --> 00:17:54,964
-How do you do?
-You care for a drink?

424
00:17:54,998 --> 00:17:56,619
I appear to be in the chair.

425
00:17:56,653 --> 00:17:58,136
Wish us bon voyage.

426
00:17:58,171 --> 00:17:59,826
-Thank you.
I'll have a Campari.

427
00:17:59,860 --> 00:18:01,998
-Truscott?
-Uh, scotch.

428
00:18:02,033 --> 00:18:04,102
-Campari and another scotch,
Hans, please.

429
00:18:04,136 --> 00:18:05,378
-Sit down, Sarah.

430
00:18:05,412 --> 00:18:06,378
-Your sister's here with you,
isn't she?

431
00:18:06,412 --> 00:18:07,584
Klein told us.
-Yes.

432
00:18:07,619 --> 00:18:10,481
-Would she care to join us?
-No.

433
00:18:10,515 --> 00:18:12,067
-Quite an occasion for me.

434
00:18:12,102 --> 00:18:13,550
My first time up a mountain.

435
00:18:13,584 --> 00:18:14,688
-Oh.
-Aren't you kind of fat

436
00:18:14,722 --> 00:18:16,412
to be climbing a mountain?

437
00:18:16,446 --> 00:18:18,688
-He's a big boy
in the Alpine Club.

438
00:18:18,722 --> 00:18:20,826
-How did you come to
take up mountaineering?

439
00:18:20,860 --> 00:18:22,446
-Wanted to see if we can't find
some good reason

440
00:18:22,481 --> 00:18:24,481
for all these accidents.

441
00:18:24,515 --> 00:18:25,826
I'm a geologist.

442
00:18:25,860 --> 00:18:28,826
I know all about rock formations
and that sort of thing.

443
00:18:28,860 --> 00:18:30,964
Well, here we are.

444
00:18:30,998 --> 00:18:32,274
Cheers.

445
00:18:32,309 --> 00:18:35,378
-Good luck.
-To a good climb.

446
00:18:35,412 --> 00:18:37,033
-Keep an eye
on your roping, won't you?

447
00:18:37,067 --> 00:18:38,378
-"Huh?"
[Ominous music plays]

448
00:18:38,412 --> 00:18:41,619
-Why roping, particularly?

449
00:18:41,653 --> 00:18:43,067
-"I don't know."
-...last week.

450
00:18:43,102 --> 00:18:44,412
-That's right. Nasty business.

451
00:18:44,446 --> 00:18:45,722
-Chill out.
-Very nasty.

452
00:18:45,757 --> 00:18:48,481
-Nasty, nasty.
-What was that?

453
00:18:48,515 --> 00:18:51,343
-Some kids, climbing
on the south col,

454
00:18:51,378 --> 00:18:52,757
and one of them fell.

455
00:18:52,791 --> 00:18:54,412
-They shouldn't have been
climbing without a guide.

456
00:18:54,446 --> 00:18:55,515
-Or shoes.

457
00:18:55,550 --> 00:18:56,619
-Seems he wasn't roped up
correctly,

458
00:18:56,653 --> 00:18:58,757
and the rope got
caught up around his neck.

459
00:18:58,791 --> 00:19:01,171
-Strangled him?
-Worse than that.

460
00:19:01,205 --> 00:19:03,929
-"Killed him."
-Tore his head off.

461
00:19:03,964 --> 00:19:05,343
-It was worse.
-"Then it strangled him."

462
00:19:05,378 --> 00:19:06,826
-You don't know the half of it.

463
00:19:06,860 --> 00:19:08,102
-"It was the fall
that killed him."

464
00:19:08,136 --> 00:19:09,550
-Tell them what the villagers
say, Hans.

465
00:19:09,584 --> 00:19:11,895
-It's not for me to...

466
00:19:11,929 --> 00:19:13,929
-What do they say, Hans?
-"That's what they say --

467
00:19:13,964 --> 00:19:15,171
'It's not for me.'"

468
00:19:15,205 --> 00:19:17,343
-The guides who found him --

469
00:19:17,378 --> 00:19:19,860
also his friends --

470
00:19:19,895 --> 00:19:22,653
they swear the rope
was around his waist.

471
00:19:22,688 --> 00:19:26,102
You understand,
it was still tied.

472
00:19:26,136 --> 00:19:29,067
-Well, but how could
it have, uh...?

473
00:19:29,102 --> 00:19:33,378
-The villagers have something
to say about that, too.

474
00:19:33,412 --> 00:19:34,584
Haven't they, Hans?

475
00:19:34,619 --> 00:19:36,171
-It was a trick knot.

476
00:19:36,205 --> 00:19:38,998
-Excuse me.

477
00:19:39,033 --> 00:19:46,826
♪♪

478
00:19:46,860 --> 00:19:48,446
-What do the villagers
say, Truscott?

479
00:19:48,481 --> 00:19:49,757
-"They say, 'It's not for me.'"

480
00:19:49,791 --> 00:19:51,412
-They say it happened
before he fell.

481
00:19:51,446 --> 00:19:53,929
-They all say that.
-As I said,

482
00:19:53,964 --> 00:19:55,481
they shouldn't allow
inexperienced climbers

483
00:19:55,515 --> 00:19:56,895
up the Trollenberg
without a guide.

484
00:19:56,929 --> 00:19:58,412
There are bound to be accidents.

485
00:19:58,446 --> 00:20:01,619
-Yes, you watch your roping.
-We ought to get moving

486
00:20:01,653 --> 00:20:03,757
if you want to make
the hut before nightfall.

487
00:20:03,791 --> 00:20:05,240
-"We've got a death scene
to make."

488
00:20:05,274 --> 00:20:07,481
-Oh, uh, I'm going up
to the observatory.

489
00:20:07,515 --> 00:20:09,205
I'll hitch a ride with you
in the cable car.

490
00:20:09,240 --> 00:20:11,102
That is, unless you're gonna
climb the whole way.

491
00:20:11,136 --> 00:20:13,412
-Climb the whole way?
Not if I can help it.

492
00:20:13,446 --> 00:20:17,619
♪♪

493
00:20:17,653 --> 00:20:19,102
-See, when an important
chemical changes,

494
00:20:19,136 --> 00:20:20,171
it can take place inside rock,

495
00:20:20,205 --> 00:20:22,826
which cause a physical
alteration

496
00:20:22,860 --> 00:20:24,274
to its structure.

497
00:20:24,309 --> 00:20:26,067
In time, it could be come
like chalk --

498
00:20:26,102 --> 00:20:27,136
break away in your hand.

499
00:20:27,171 --> 00:20:28,619
-What do you think
of that, Brett?

500
00:20:28,653 --> 00:20:30,240
-Hmm?

501
00:20:30,274 --> 00:20:31,998
No, I don't think
it's like that.

502
00:20:32,033 --> 00:20:33,584
A mountain's a mountain.

503
00:20:33,619 --> 00:20:35,550
Some people can climb it,
and some can't.

504
00:20:35,584 --> 00:20:37,412
-Some people can speak dialogue,some can't.

505
00:20:37,446 --> 00:20:39,446
-Those that can't
shouldn't try.

506
00:20:39,481 --> 00:20:41,860
-I'm here under sufferance.

507
00:20:41,895 --> 00:20:43,378
-How long do you think
it'll take you to reach the hut

508
00:20:43,412 --> 00:20:44,481
from the observatory?

509
00:20:44,515 --> 00:20:47,378
-I don't know.
-Oh, about 3 1/2 hours.

510
00:20:47,412 --> 00:20:48,653
An easy climb.

511
00:20:48,688 --> 00:20:50,378
-"That's good.
I'm wearing a sport coat."

512
00:20:50,412 --> 00:20:58,240
♪♪

513
00:20:58,274 --> 00:21:01,895
Meanwhile, in the observatory...

514
00:21:01,929 --> 00:21:05,067
-Professor.
Professor.

515
00:21:05,102 --> 00:21:08,067
-How many more times do I tellyou, I am not to be interrupted.

516
00:21:08,102 --> 00:21:10,102
-I'm sorry, sir, but there's
someone outside to see you.

517
00:21:10,136 --> 00:21:11,584
-I don't care.
Tell them to go away.

518
00:21:11,619 --> 00:21:13,653
-He says his name is Brooks.
-Tell him to --

519
00:21:13,688 --> 00:21:15,412
-"Huh?"

520
00:21:15,446 --> 00:21:17,481
-Alan Brooks?
-Yes, sir.

521
00:21:17,515 --> 00:21:19,378
-Well, bring him in,
bring him in!

522
00:21:19,412 --> 00:21:21,136
Now, wait, wait!

523
00:21:21,171 --> 00:21:22,378
I'll bring him in myself.

524
00:21:22,412 --> 00:21:23,964
-"No, wait, wait.
You get him.

525
00:21:23,998 --> 00:21:25,378
No, I'll get him.

526
00:21:25,412 --> 00:21:26,791
Uh, yeah, I'll get him.

527
00:21:26,826 --> 00:21:28,929
I have to do
everything around here."

528
00:21:28,964 --> 00:21:31,412
-Alan.
My dear Alan!

529
00:21:31,446 --> 00:21:32,584
Ha ha!

530
00:21:32,619 --> 00:21:34,481
They all get that for leaving
you out here!

531
00:21:34,515 --> 00:21:35,722
Come in, come in.

532
00:21:35,757 --> 00:21:37,412
Ah, it's good of you to come
and see me,

533
00:21:37,446 --> 00:21:39,067
and on your holidays, too.

534
00:21:39,102 --> 00:21:40,515
I was lucky you were in Europe.

535
00:21:40,550 --> 00:21:42,481
You got my letter?
-Yes.

536
00:21:42,515 --> 00:21:44,412
-Well, you don't say very much.
What's the matter?

537
00:21:44,446 --> 00:21:46,205
You're not pleased to see
your old friend?

538
00:21:46,240 --> 00:21:48,171
-Wie geht, Herr Professor?

539
00:21:48,205 --> 00:21:49,860
-And how are you, Alan?

540
00:21:49,895 --> 00:21:51,964
Come in, come in!

541
00:21:51,998 --> 00:21:53,515
-What a goof.

542
00:21:53,550 --> 00:21:57,067
Well, Alan, what do you think
of our little observatory, eh?

543
00:21:57,102 --> 00:21:59,067
-Groovy pad.

544
00:21:59,102 --> 00:22:03,274
-You know, the government gives
me as much money as I want.

545
00:22:03,309 --> 00:22:04,826
-Very retro.
Needs some more color, though.

546
00:22:04,860 --> 00:22:09,136
-I'll show you something.

547
00:22:09,171 --> 00:22:10,309
-Television?

548
00:22:10,343 --> 00:22:12,929
-Better than windows.
So, look.

549
00:22:16,171 --> 00:22:17,929
-Look --
the all-weather channel.

550
00:22:17,964 --> 00:22:20,412
These are mountains you won't
see on any other station.

551
00:22:20,446 --> 00:22:22,895
-You see?
Television cameras on the roof.

552
00:22:22,929 --> 00:22:24,550
We watch everywhere.

553
00:22:28,550 --> 00:22:30,309
[Chuckles]

554
00:22:32,860 --> 00:22:34,757
You know, the government --

555
00:22:34,791 --> 00:22:36,102
the government, they say to me,

556
00:22:36,136 --> 00:22:39,550
"Professor, do you have to have
such expensive things?

557
00:22:39,584 --> 00:22:41,688
Windows are much cheaper."

558
00:22:41,722 --> 00:22:44,791
And I say, "I have to have,"
and I have!

559
00:22:44,826 --> 00:22:46,722
-Do you think that's funny?

560
00:22:46,757 --> 00:22:47,826
-That over there,
that is the only window.

561
00:22:47,860 --> 00:22:49,998
-Science humor.

562
00:22:50,033 --> 00:22:52,584
-And even from that,
we have protection.

563
00:22:52,619 --> 00:22:54,378
Look.

564
00:22:54,412 --> 00:22:56,964
-"Now the windows
are even smaller.

565
00:22:56,998 --> 00:22:59,515
But I can make them big again."

566
00:22:59,550 --> 00:23:00,619
-Could just get roller blinds.

567
00:23:00,653 --> 00:23:02,412
-That will stand up
to any avalanche.

568
00:23:02,446 --> 00:23:04,998
[Chuckles]

569
00:23:05,033 --> 00:23:08,412
But that, too, was very
expensive.

570
00:23:08,446 --> 00:23:11,343
All this to study cosmic rays.

571
00:23:11,378 --> 00:23:15,378
Well, Alan, come
into my office, huh?

572
00:23:15,412 --> 00:23:19,378
Well, Alan, here we are.

573
00:23:19,412 --> 00:23:20,964
Same old mess, huh?

574
00:23:20,998 --> 00:23:22,619
-"But it's an expensive mess."

575
00:23:22,653 --> 00:23:25,929
-Well, you're looking very fit.

576
00:23:25,964 --> 00:23:29,067
-Well, I look
the same as always.

577
00:23:29,102 --> 00:23:30,412
Come on, quit your stalling,
Professor.

578
00:23:30,446 --> 00:23:31,826
You forget I know you.

579
00:23:31,860 --> 00:23:34,757
You said in your letter that
I should see you right away.

580
00:23:34,791 --> 00:23:36,309
What's so urgent?

581
00:23:36,343 --> 00:23:38,240
-How long have you been here
in Trollenberg, Alan?

582
00:23:38,274 --> 00:23:39,998
-Got here this morning,
came right up to see you.

583
00:23:40,033 --> 00:23:41,274
-"So you haven't tried
the fish yet."

584
00:23:41,309 --> 00:23:43,550
-You haven't heard about
the accidents, then?

585
00:23:43,584 --> 00:23:45,412
-Yes, I heard
about the students.

586
00:23:45,446 --> 00:23:47,757
-Yeah, that was one of them,
but...

587
00:23:47,791 --> 00:23:50,412
But there's been others --
many others.

588
00:23:50,446 --> 00:23:54,309
-Where people climb mountains,
there are lots of accidents.

589
00:23:54,343 --> 00:23:55,584
-That's true.

590
00:23:55,619 --> 00:23:58,826
And sometimes, the bodies,
they disappear.

591
00:23:58,860 --> 00:24:01,998
But here, the search parties
go out,

592
00:24:02,033 --> 00:24:04,619
and always they find nothing.

593
00:24:04,653 --> 00:24:05,653
Now, why is that?

594
00:24:05,688 --> 00:24:07,619
-They're not good
search parties?

595
00:24:07,653 --> 00:24:10,136
-But they're expensive.

596
00:24:10,171 --> 00:24:12,446
-I don't know.

597
00:24:12,481 --> 00:24:14,860
-And then, there is the cloud.

598
00:24:14,895 --> 00:24:16,722
-What cloud?

599
00:24:16,757 --> 00:24:18,998
-Come on, Alan, you know
what I'm talking about --

600
00:24:19,033 --> 00:24:20,550
the cloud
where there should be no cloud.

601
00:24:20,584 --> 00:24:21,826
-"Oh, yeah, oh,
yeah, that cloud."

602
00:24:21,860 --> 00:24:23,895
-Where there are mountains,
there are always clouds.

603
00:24:23,929 --> 00:24:26,998
-But this one remains static
on the side of the Trollenberg.

604
00:24:27,033 --> 00:24:28,515
It never moves.
-Oh, right.

605
00:24:28,550 --> 00:24:31,550
-A freak of nature.

606
00:24:31,584 --> 00:24:34,653
-Hey.
-A radioactive freak of nature?

607
00:24:34,688 --> 00:24:37,102
-Radioactive?
-Mm-hmm.

608
00:24:37,136 --> 00:24:39,274
-Can we see it from here?
-Come.

609
00:24:39,309 --> 00:24:40,757
I'll show you.

610
00:24:40,791 --> 00:24:45,619
[Motors whirring]

611
00:24:50,240 --> 00:24:53,102
-Okay. What did you guys think
of the movie so far?

612
00:24:53,136 --> 00:24:55,033
-Well, I thought
there'd be more music.

613
00:24:55,067 --> 00:24:58,033
You know, more of
a Julie Andrews quality.

614
00:24:58,067 --> 00:24:59,171
Ow.
-Julie Andrews quality?

615
00:24:59,205 --> 00:25:01,033
Isn't that a contradiction
in terms?

616
00:25:01,067 --> 00:25:02,343
-Now, come on, Crow.

617
00:25:02,378 --> 00:25:04,688
What did you think
of the movie so far?

618
00:25:04,722 --> 00:25:06,136
-Well, Joel, I can't understand

619
00:25:06,171 --> 00:25:08,619
why everybody's so upset
about losing their heads.

620
00:25:08,653 --> 00:25:10,067
What's so bad about that?

621
00:25:10,102 --> 00:25:13,343
I've seen Servo's head
on the workbench lots of times.

622
00:25:13,378 --> 00:25:15,067
-Yeah, screws right off.
It's a pop top.

623
00:25:15,102 --> 00:25:16,412
-Ew.

624
00:25:16,446 --> 00:25:17,998
-Well, listen,
it's not like that.

625
00:25:18,033 --> 00:25:19,378
It's really --

626
00:25:19,412 --> 00:25:21,171
Human beings are made
completely different, you guys.

627
00:25:21,205 --> 00:25:23,964
Once our heads are separated
from our bodies,

628
00:25:23,998 --> 00:25:25,757
that's pretty much
the end of the show for us.

629
00:25:25,791 --> 00:25:27,067
It's just the way we're made.

630
00:25:27,102 --> 00:25:30,274
-Can't you use your backup
copy and reboot?

631
00:25:30,309 --> 00:25:33,136
-Uh, no.
Nope, nothing like that.

632
00:25:33,171 --> 00:25:35,929
-Well, then why do people say
that they're always like,

633
00:25:35,964 --> 00:25:38,240
"I'd lose my head
if it wasn't screwed on"?

634
00:25:38,274 --> 00:25:39,446
-Yeah, and people often say

635
00:25:39,481 --> 00:25:41,171
their heads
aren't in the right places.

636
00:25:41,205 --> 00:25:43,205
-Yeah, and, Joel, once I heard
the scientists talking,

637
00:25:43,240 --> 00:25:44,722
and they said you had
your head up your a--

638
00:25:44,757 --> 00:25:49,412
-Uh, well, Crow, that was just
a figure of speech, all right?

639
00:25:49,446 --> 00:25:50,412
-Figure of speech?

640
00:25:50,446 --> 00:25:51,722
Now, what's that
supposed to mean?

641
00:25:51,757 --> 00:25:53,722
-Oh, like the body
of a paragraph, perhaps?

642
00:25:53,757 --> 00:25:55,619
-I think that's
a literary figure.

643
00:25:55,653 --> 00:25:58,343
-Oh, maybe like
Edna St. Vincent Millay?

644
00:25:58,378 --> 00:25:59,929
-Now there's a body!

645
00:25:59,964 --> 00:26:01,205
-Now listen, you guys,

646
00:26:01,240 --> 00:26:04,274
I'm trying to
teach you something, all right?

647
00:26:04,309 --> 00:26:06,826
-Joel, what about head games?
-And head trips?

648
00:26:06,860 --> 00:26:08,584
-And how do you
explain head cheese?

649
00:26:08,619 --> 00:26:11,757
-Well, uh, head cheese --
I don't explain head cheese.

650
00:26:11,791 --> 00:26:14,481
But, uh, here are
some people who do.

651
00:26:14,515 --> 00:26:19,515
♪♪

652
00:26:27,171 --> 00:26:29,102
-Joel, will you carry me
everywhere from now on?

653
00:26:29,136 --> 00:26:30,757
-I gotta put wheels
on you sometime.

654
00:26:30,791 --> 00:26:33,171
-Hey, look! It's that painting
we saw earlier.

655
00:26:36,619 --> 00:26:39,757
-Alan, come here.
-"Hey, wait for me.

656
00:26:39,791 --> 00:26:41,378
Hey!"

657
00:26:41,412 --> 00:26:44,274
-"Keep up."

658
00:26:44,309 --> 00:26:47,860
-Now, you see, here --
here's a map of the area.

659
00:26:47,895 --> 00:26:49,274
-"Admittedly,
it's not a very good map."

660
00:26:49,309 --> 00:26:52,067
-The Trollenberg.
-Yep, that's Trollenberg.

661
00:26:52,102 --> 00:26:54,446
-That is the scan
atop of the roof here.

662
00:26:54,481 --> 00:26:56,309
And you see, at the moment,
nothing.

663
00:26:56,343 --> 00:26:57,998
No reaction, nothing.

664
00:26:58,033 --> 00:27:01,929
Now, remember,
here is the cloud.

665
00:27:01,964 --> 00:27:03,412
I turn on the scanner.

666
00:27:03,446 --> 00:27:06,171
So, now watch
when it passes the cloud.

667
00:27:06,205 --> 00:27:07,515
-Where's the cloud?

668
00:27:07,550 --> 00:27:09,998
Where's the cloud again?

669
00:27:10,033 --> 00:27:13,240
-Now, you see,
it passes the cloud, nothing.

670
00:27:13,274 --> 00:27:16,309
Watch it go 'round again.
-Where's the cloud?

671
00:27:16,343 --> 00:27:18,584
Where is it? Ah.
[Sputtering]

672
00:27:18,619 --> 00:27:21,033
-It's a cloud of baked beans,
I think.

673
00:27:21,067 --> 00:27:23,998
-Sounds like it, right, Tom?
-Sure does.

674
00:27:26,033 --> 00:27:28,309
-Well, what do you
think of that?

675
00:27:31,240 --> 00:27:35,102
Now, Alan, I ask you to think
back three years ago,

676
00:27:35,136 --> 00:27:37,653
to what happened in the Andes.

677
00:27:37,688 --> 00:27:39,653
-Well --
Well, this can't be the same.

678
00:27:39,688 --> 00:27:41,102
-Why not?

679
00:27:41,136 --> 00:27:43,550
They have accidents like before,we have the cloud like before.

680
00:27:43,584 --> 00:27:44,653
Why can't it be the same?

681
00:27:44,688 --> 00:27:46,343
-There's too many
things missing.

682
00:27:46,378 --> 00:27:47,343
-Like a plot.

683
00:27:47,378 --> 00:27:49,446
-Mental compulsion.
-What is it?

684
00:27:49,481 --> 00:27:54,412
-This girl on the train.
She --

685
00:27:54,446 --> 00:27:57,584
She suddenly decided she had
to get off at Trollenberg.

686
00:27:57,619 --> 00:27:59,791
She was booked for Geneva.

687
00:27:59,826 --> 00:28:03,274
But she had to get off
at Trollenberg.

688
00:28:03,309 --> 00:28:05,722
-And did she?
-Yes.

689
00:28:05,757 --> 00:28:08,033
-She had to.
-She's at the hotel.

690
00:28:08,067 --> 00:28:09,895
-There's climbers
on the Trollenberg.

691
00:28:11,998 --> 00:28:12,964
-Climbers?

692
00:28:12,998 --> 00:28:15,929
I thought they were
all scared away.

693
00:28:15,964 --> 00:28:17,033
Where are they?

694
00:28:17,067 --> 00:28:20,653
-60 degrees west of due north.

695
00:28:20,688 --> 00:28:23,309
-Hey, it's "F Troop."

696
00:28:23,343 --> 00:28:25,378
-[Whistling]

697
00:28:25,412 --> 00:28:27,067
-2,000 meters.

698
00:28:27,102 --> 00:28:28,378
-[Whistling]

699
00:28:32,895 --> 00:28:34,688
-Two guys.

700
00:28:34,722 --> 00:28:36,136
Hey.
-Captain Parmenter.

701
00:28:36,171 --> 00:28:37,343
-It's Brett and Dewhurst.

702
00:28:37,378 --> 00:28:38,619
-"Isn't he too fat to be
climbing a mountain?"

703
00:28:38,653 --> 00:28:40,619
-You know them?
-Well, just from the hotel.

704
00:28:40,653 --> 00:28:42,619
They came up with me
on the cable car.

705
00:28:46,722 --> 00:28:48,446
-Well, they should be all right

706
00:28:48,481 --> 00:28:50,446
as long as they stick
to their present track.

707
00:28:50,481 --> 00:28:52,102
-And they keep their heads
on their necks.

708
00:28:52,136 --> 00:28:54,205
-The cloud is well
to the west of the pass.

709
00:28:54,240 --> 00:28:55,722
-Mm.

710
00:28:55,757 --> 00:28:58,274
-Alan...

711
00:28:58,309 --> 00:29:00,860
will you do something
about what I told you?

712
00:29:00,895 --> 00:29:02,481
-The cloud?
-Mm.

713
00:29:02,515 --> 00:29:04,136
-Do what?

714
00:29:04,171 --> 00:29:05,929
-Inform the authorities.

715
00:29:05,964 --> 00:29:07,860
-Look, I'm on a holiday.

716
00:29:07,895 --> 00:29:11,343
Besides, you haven't told me
anything that can be proved.

717
00:29:11,378 --> 00:29:12,412
-I've told you about the cloud,

718
00:29:12,446 --> 00:29:13,550
I've told you
about the accidents.

719
00:29:13,584 --> 00:29:15,309
What more do you want?
-Facts, proof.

720
00:29:15,343 --> 00:29:16,584
-Scotch.
-Something that'll look real

721
00:29:16,619 --> 00:29:19,378
in black-and-white
on somebody's desk.

722
00:29:19,412 --> 00:29:20,343
I don't know.

723
00:29:20,378 --> 00:29:21,515
Not gonna stick my neck
out again

724
00:29:21,550 --> 00:29:23,102
like I did in the Andes.

725
00:29:23,136 --> 00:29:24,929
-But why?

726
00:29:24,964 --> 00:29:27,412
You were employed
by the United Nations.

727
00:29:27,446 --> 00:29:28,550
It was all in the report.

728
00:29:28,584 --> 00:29:30,205
-Look, by the time the Andes
report was in,

729
00:29:30,240 --> 00:29:31,964
there wasn't anything left
in the area,

730
00:29:31,998 --> 00:29:33,584
explained or otherwise.

731
00:29:33,619 --> 00:29:37,550
They practically accused me
of dreaming the whole thing up.

732
00:29:37,584 --> 00:29:38,964
So if I was to take a hand here,

733
00:29:38,998 --> 00:29:41,412
I'd have to have a list
of documented facts.

734
00:29:41,446 --> 00:29:42,929
They'd have to be
pretty conclusive.

735
00:29:42,964 --> 00:29:44,274
-"Or I'll look like
a dork again."

736
00:29:44,309 --> 00:29:45,550
-You're an important man.

737
00:29:45,584 --> 00:29:47,033
Why don't you
get through to Bern?

738
00:29:47,067 --> 00:29:48,584
-"Important."

739
00:29:48,619 --> 00:29:52,033
I'm only important if I say
something about cosmic rays.

740
00:29:52,067 --> 00:29:55,446
If I say anything else, theytell me to mind my own business.

741
00:29:55,481 --> 00:29:56,791
-Why don't they give him
something to study

742
00:29:56,826 --> 00:29:58,205
that he can pronounce?

743
00:29:58,240 --> 00:30:00,860
-Maybe you should
speak to Klein.

744
00:30:00,895 --> 00:30:02,240
-Hotel proprietor?

745
00:30:02,274 --> 00:30:03,929
-Well, he's also the mayor
of Trollenberg.

746
00:30:03,964 --> 00:30:05,964
Perhaps he could help.

747
00:30:05,998 --> 00:30:09,274
Anyway, he could supply
a list of the accidents.

748
00:30:09,309 --> 00:30:10,584
-He might be more interesting.

749
00:30:10,619 --> 00:30:12,791
-Then perhaps you do
something yourself.

750
00:30:12,826 --> 00:30:15,171
-Oh.
[Wind howling]

751
00:30:15,205 --> 00:30:17,343
-Meanwhile, back
at Daniel Boone's house...

752
00:30:19,378 --> 00:30:22,653
-"Damn. I'm just too fat to be
climbing a mountain."

753
00:30:22,688 --> 00:30:24,550
[Joel chuckles]

754
00:30:24,584 --> 00:30:27,757
-"What a nice place."
-"Gee, there's only one bed.

755
00:30:27,791 --> 00:30:29,826
Guess we'll have
to flip for it."

756
00:30:29,860 --> 00:30:31,688
-He's too fat to flip.

757
00:30:31,722 --> 00:30:33,757
-"Hmm, let me see.

758
00:30:33,791 --> 00:30:35,412
I could kill the fat guy
with this.

759
00:30:35,446 --> 00:30:37,205
Nope, too flimsy.

760
00:30:37,240 --> 00:30:38,412
Yeah, this is it!

761
00:30:38,446 --> 00:30:39,688
Nice and hefty."

762
00:30:39,722 --> 00:30:42,757
-You all right?

763
00:30:42,791 --> 00:30:44,205
All right,
take your pack off, man.

764
00:30:44,240 --> 00:30:45,860
You can't relax like that.

765
00:30:53,240 --> 00:30:56,033
I'll call Weight Watchers.
[Ringing]

766
00:30:56,067 --> 00:30:58,378
-Hello?

767
00:30:58,412 --> 00:31:00,826
Yes, Herr Brett.

768
00:31:00,860 --> 00:31:02,722
You arrived all right?
-"Uh-huh."

769
00:31:02,757 --> 00:31:04,446
-Good.

770
00:31:04,481 --> 00:31:06,619
Good.

771
00:31:06,653 --> 00:31:10,102
A nice sleep will set you right.

772
00:31:10,136 --> 00:31:11,998
-Yes, all right.
-"Okay."

773
00:31:12,033 --> 00:31:13,826
-Goodbye.
-"Thanks a lot, bye."

774
00:31:16,274 --> 00:31:17,757
-It was Herr Brett.

775
00:31:17,791 --> 00:31:19,860
They have arrived at the hut.

776
00:31:19,895 --> 00:31:22,757
You see?
There's nothing to worry about.

777
00:31:22,791 --> 00:31:24,136
It's just unfortunate

778
00:31:24,171 --> 00:31:28,205
that this year we have had
many inexperienced climbers.

779
00:31:28,240 --> 00:31:30,205
-And what about the rumors
in the village?

780
00:31:30,240 --> 00:31:32,826
-Well, you know these people,
Professor.

781
00:31:32,860 --> 00:31:34,722
They are superstitious.

782
00:31:34,757 --> 00:31:38,067
They like to believe
in fairy stories.

783
00:31:38,102 --> 00:31:40,309
Gentlemen, you understand.

784
00:31:40,343 --> 00:31:43,446
Officially,
there's nothing I can do.

785
00:31:43,481 --> 00:31:46,619
-"Except sit here
and drink heavily."

786
00:31:46,653 --> 00:31:49,412
-"Dear Diary, once again
the fat guy got the bed."

787
00:31:49,446 --> 00:31:50,998
-...back tomorrow,

788
00:31:51,033 --> 00:31:52,274
if we travel light.

789
00:31:52,309 --> 00:31:55,757
-I must have a sack
for rock samples.

790
00:31:55,791 --> 00:31:58,309
-Well, I think
we can manage that.

791
00:31:58,343 --> 00:31:59,964
You can take your sack.

792
00:31:59,998 --> 00:32:06,688
♪♪

793
00:32:06,722 --> 00:32:08,653
-"Penny for your thoughts.

794
00:32:08,688 --> 00:32:12,171
Think we'll ever get married?"

795
00:32:12,205 --> 00:32:14,033
-Visibility's not too good.

796
00:32:14,067 --> 00:32:15,998
Hope it'll clear.

797
00:32:16,033 --> 00:32:17,688
-Fog?
Out here?

798
00:32:20,102 --> 00:32:21,481
-"Oh, it's just a painting.
Sorry.

799
00:32:21,515 --> 00:32:24,826
My mistake."

800
00:32:24,860 --> 00:32:26,860
-Moving down from the peak.

801
00:32:28,619 --> 00:32:30,653
-Will this do, Miss Pilgrim?

802
00:32:30,688 --> 00:32:32,343
-Oh, this will be fine.

803
00:32:35,343 --> 00:32:37,998
Quite a collection
you have there.

804
00:32:38,033 --> 00:32:39,412
Now, would somebody
bring Anne in, please?

805
00:32:39,446 --> 00:32:40,826
-Oh, yes.

806
00:32:40,860 --> 00:32:42,171
-Do we have to?
-Now, you see that I make

807
00:32:42,205 --> 00:32:43,343
no signals to Anne
and say nothing.

808
00:32:43,378 --> 00:32:44,619
-Anne?
-So it's impossible

809
00:32:44,653 --> 00:32:46,446
for any sort of code to be used.

810
00:32:49,929 --> 00:32:54,964
[Clearing throat loudly]

811
00:32:58,446 --> 00:33:00,446
[Clearing throat loudly]

812
00:33:00,481 --> 00:33:03,274
-The bank note is French.
-"It's a mango."

813
00:33:03,309 --> 00:33:07,136
-500 francs.
-"Mm-hmm, mm-hmm."

814
00:33:07,171 --> 00:33:10,205
-There's a number on it.
-"Mm-hmm, mm-hmm."

815
00:33:14,860 --> 00:33:16,446
-H...

816
00:33:16,481 --> 00:33:18,102
0...

817
00:33:18,136 --> 00:33:21,895
1...
1...

818
00:33:21,929 --> 00:33:24,550
8...

819
00:33:24,584 --> 00:33:25,688
8.

820
00:33:25,722 --> 00:33:28,688
-Well done.

821
00:33:28,722 --> 00:33:31,067
-And who does it belong to,
young lady?

822
00:33:31,102 --> 00:33:32,895
-[Coughing] "Roberts!
Roberts!"

823
00:33:35,964 --> 00:33:39,102
-"Pretty good."

824
00:33:39,136 --> 00:33:41,171
-It's a rounded sort of object

825
00:33:41,205 --> 00:33:43,343
about the size of a tennis ball.

826
00:33:43,378 --> 00:33:46,171
-"Paperweight, paperweight."
-Made of glass.

827
00:33:46,205 --> 00:33:48,412
I think it's used
as a paperweight.

828
00:33:48,446 --> 00:33:53,309
-"Good, good."
-There's a model inside it.

829
00:33:53,343 --> 00:33:56,722
A mountain and a little hut.

830
00:33:56,757 --> 00:33:59,205
And when you shake it,
there's a snowstorm.

831
00:33:59,240 --> 00:34:01,619
-"When you shake it,
the heads come off."

832
00:34:01,653 --> 00:34:03,102
-"Rosebud."

833
00:34:03,136 --> 00:34:05,412
[Chatter]

834
00:34:05,446 --> 00:34:09,343
-No.

835
00:34:09,378 --> 00:34:10,929
Mountain...

836
00:34:10,964 --> 00:34:14,067
hut...

837
00:34:14,102 --> 00:34:15,343
Two men in the hut.

838
00:34:15,378 --> 00:34:19,446
-Anne!
-"The fat one takes the bed."

839
00:34:19,481 --> 00:34:22,584
-There's the fat one.
He's asleep.

840
00:34:22,619 --> 00:34:26,274
-"In the bed."
-But he is not the one.

841
00:34:26,309 --> 00:34:27,929
The other one...

842
00:34:30,102 --> 00:34:32,309
looking at the table.

843
00:34:32,343 --> 00:34:34,791
-"Dreaming...
doubting."

844
00:34:34,826 --> 00:34:37,274
-"Writing in his diary."

845
00:34:37,309 --> 00:34:39,309
-He's the one.

846
00:34:39,343 --> 00:34:43,929
[Ominous music playing]

847
00:34:43,964 --> 00:34:46,757
Getting up.

848
00:34:46,791 --> 00:34:48,619
Coming towards the door.

849
00:34:48,653 --> 00:34:50,722
-Maybe she's not psychic.
Maybe she's controlling him.

850
00:34:50,757 --> 00:34:54,102
If she shuts up,
maybe he'll sit down.

851
00:34:54,136 --> 00:34:55,757
-He's opening it.

852
00:34:59,033 --> 00:35:01,343
He's coming out.

853
00:35:01,378 --> 00:35:03,309
Up the slope.

854
00:35:03,343 --> 00:35:05,964
Up the slope.
-Flash!

855
00:35:05,998 --> 00:35:08,378
Mr. and Mrs. America
and all ships at sea!

856
00:35:08,412 --> 00:35:10,412
This is a banner extra,
front-page stuff!

857
00:35:10,446 --> 00:35:13,998
"Girl has vision!"
-Mountain...

858
00:35:14,033 --> 00:35:16,309
Paperweight.

859
00:35:16,343 --> 00:35:17,964
Glass paperweight.

860
00:35:19,619 --> 00:35:21,584
-"Dude, she's mine.

861
00:35:21,619 --> 00:35:23,102
I'll get her arm."

862
00:35:25,584 --> 00:35:27,584
[Telephone ringing]

863
00:35:34,515 --> 00:35:36,205
-Hello?

864
00:35:36,240 --> 00:35:38,929
-Hello, Brett?
-Dewhurst here.

865
00:35:38,964 --> 00:35:41,309
What do you want?
-Are you all right?

866
00:35:41,343 --> 00:35:43,550
-Yes.
Why?

867
00:35:43,584 --> 00:35:45,757
-Is Brett there?
-Of course he is.

868
00:35:45,791 --> 00:35:48,033
Wouldn't go wandering off
in the middle of the night.

869
00:35:48,067 --> 00:35:50,067
-"Hmm?"
-Wait, hold on.

870
00:35:52,481 --> 00:35:54,515
-"Now I'm fat and awake.
Who keeps calling me?

871
00:35:54,550 --> 00:35:56,446
Thanks for calling me, jerk."

872
00:36:02,067 --> 00:36:03,446
-Dewhurst!

873
00:36:03,481 --> 00:36:05,240
Dewhurst, is Brett there?
Can you hear me?

874
00:36:05,274 --> 00:36:07,171
Dewhurst!
Are you there?

875
00:36:07,205 --> 00:36:08,171
Hello, hello?!

876
00:36:08,205 --> 00:36:09,998
-"Now I have to walk
back over here."

877
00:36:10,033 --> 00:36:11,136
-Hello?
-"Could you order me a pizza?"

878
00:36:11,171 --> 00:36:12,412
-You're right.
He's not here.

879
00:36:12,446 --> 00:36:14,102
He must've just gone out.

880
00:36:14,136 --> 00:36:16,791
I better go look for him.
-No, don't do that.

881
00:36:16,826 --> 00:36:18,136
You'll be lost in 5 seconds.

882
00:36:18,171 --> 00:36:20,102
Just sit tight where you are.
-I think I should go.

883
00:36:20,136 --> 00:36:22,309
There may be something wrong.

884
00:36:22,343 --> 00:36:23,929
-No, you're not to move.
Stay in the hut!

885
00:36:23,964 --> 00:36:25,171
What's the weather like?

886
00:36:25,205 --> 00:36:27,550
-Not very good.
Some heavy cloud coming down.

887
00:36:27,584 --> 00:36:28,895
-Well, then stay where you are.

888
00:36:28,929 --> 00:36:30,584
There'll be somebody at this endof the phone all night.

889
00:36:30,619 --> 00:36:33,584
If Brett shows up or if youneed us, ring us here, will you?

890
00:36:33,619 --> 00:36:35,102
-All right.
Bye.

891
00:36:35,136 --> 00:36:36,653
-Bye.

892
00:36:41,550 --> 00:36:43,722
-"Well, let's see.
Something to hold me over."

893
00:36:43,757 --> 00:36:45,240
[Humming]

894
00:36:45,274 --> 00:36:50,171
"Ah, can't eat that
or it'll just make me hungrier.

895
00:36:50,205 --> 00:36:51,826
Maybe the blanket."

896
00:36:54,446 --> 00:36:56,067
-Brett?!

897
00:36:57,102 --> 00:36:59,102
Brett?!

898
00:37:00,515 --> 00:37:02,171
-"Echo!"

899
00:37:15,033 --> 00:37:17,171
-"It was a bizarre dream.

900
00:37:17,205 --> 00:37:18,412
And you were all there."

901
00:37:18,446 --> 00:37:19,481
-Why? What happened?

902
00:37:19,515 --> 00:37:22,033
-"Fanny Flag and Groucho
and Carl Sagan,

903
00:37:22,067 --> 00:37:24,619
and it was
a Dick Cavett PBS special."

904
00:37:24,653 --> 00:37:26,343
-Well, we need something
to relax your sister.

905
00:37:26,378 --> 00:37:28,067
A sedative perhaps.

906
00:37:28,102 --> 00:37:29,274
You have such a thing?

907
00:37:29,309 --> 00:37:30,895
-I may have some sleeping pills
in my room.

908
00:37:30,929 --> 00:37:31,895
Shall I see if I can find them?

909
00:37:31,929 --> 00:37:33,136
-Oh, please, thank you.

910
00:37:41,653 --> 00:37:44,895
Sarah, could I speak with you
for a minute, please?

911
00:37:47,757 --> 00:37:49,067
-All right.

912
00:37:49,102 --> 00:37:50,998
[High-pitched whine]

913
00:37:51,033 --> 00:37:52,515
I shan't be a minute.

914
00:37:52,550 --> 00:37:53,929
-What's that noise?

915
00:37:53,964 --> 00:37:56,378
-It's like someone sharpening
a pencil or something.

916
00:37:56,412 --> 00:37:58,274
-I think it's
the electric blanket.

917
00:38:03,343 --> 00:38:07,136
-Oh, Miss Sarah, I don't
want you to upset yourself.

918
00:38:07,171 --> 00:38:08,171
-Are these any good?

919
00:38:08,205 --> 00:38:10,309
-He prescribed those for Elvis.

920
00:38:10,343 --> 00:38:11,964
-Yes, it's fine.

921
00:38:11,998 --> 00:38:14,653
Now, you give her two of these,
then come downstairs.

922
00:38:14,688 --> 00:38:15,998
Please hurry.

923
00:38:16,033 --> 00:38:17,619
-All right.

924
00:38:22,309 --> 00:38:23,481
-"Is it my breath?"

925
00:38:23,515 --> 00:38:27,653
[Theme music playing]

926
00:38:30,033 --> 00:38:34,446
[Telephone ringing]

927
00:38:34,481 --> 00:38:35,826
-Hello?

928
00:38:35,860 --> 00:38:38,860
-"My pizza ready?
It's been over a half-hour."

929
00:38:38,895 --> 00:38:41,481
-It's foggy as hell outside.

930
00:38:41,515 --> 00:38:43,584
You know I can't see far.

931
00:38:43,619 --> 00:38:46,067
Perishing cold, too.

932
00:38:46,102 --> 00:38:49,343
Yes. Yes.

933
00:38:49,378 --> 00:38:51,860
Hold on a minute.

934
00:38:51,895 --> 00:38:53,274
There's someone outside.
Hang on.

935
00:38:53,309 --> 00:38:55,102
-Maybe it's the neighbor kids.

936
00:38:55,136 --> 00:38:58,895
-Could be the pizza man.
-"Man, get out of my flowers."

937
00:38:58,929 --> 00:39:01,136
-It's the stealth pizza man.

938
00:39:01,171 --> 00:39:02,446
-Brett?

939
00:39:02,481 --> 00:39:04,067
Brett, is that you?

940
00:39:04,102 --> 00:39:08,757
♪♪

941
00:39:08,791 --> 00:39:12,550
False alarm, I guess.

942
00:39:12,584 --> 00:39:14,412
Hang on a minute.I've just got to close the door.

943
00:39:14,446 --> 00:39:21,584
♪♪

944
00:39:21,619 --> 00:39:24,240
Aah!

945
00:39:24,274 --> 00:39:27,688
-It's the Noid.

946
00:39:27,722 --> 00:39:29,412
-Do we ever get to see
the monster, Joel?

947
00:39:29,446 --> 00:39:30,998
-Dewhurst!
Can you hear me?

948
00:39:31,033 --> 00:39:35,033
Dewhurst!
Dewhurst?!

949
00:39:35,067 --> 00:39:36,378
Hello, hello?!

950
00:39:36,412 --> 00:39:37,619
Dewhurst!

951
00:39:37,653 --> 00:39:40,964
[Dewhurst screaming]

952
00:39:40,998 --> 00:39:43,033
-"Anchovies."
-Well?

953
00:39:43,067 --> 00:39:44,274
-"He said anchovies."

954
00:39:44,309 --> 00:39:45,653
-Phone's gone dead.

955
00:39:45,688 --> 00:39:49,205
[Telephone ringing]

956
00:39:49,240 --> 00:39:51,653
-That one hasn't.

957
00:39:51,688 --> 00:39:53,205
-Hello?

958
00:39:53,240 --> 00:39:55,136
Yes, speaking.

959
00:39:55,171 --> 00:39:56,274
Yes.

960
00:39:56,309 --> 00:39:57,481
-It's Harpo.

961
00:39:57,515 --> 00:39:59,412
-I see.

962
00:39:59,446 --> 00:40:00,929
-"Hmm?"

963
00:40:00,964 --> 00:40:04,102
-All right.
All right.

964
00:40:04,136 --> 00:40:05,343
-He said anchovies.

965
00:40:08,895 --> 00:40:11,136
-It was the observatory.

966
00:40:11,171 --> 00:40:14,446
The cloud has moved away
from the hut.

967
00:40:14,481 --> 00:40:16,033
Back up the Trollenberg.
[Bell ringing]

968
00:40:16,067 --> 00:40:18,515
-Oh, nice reel change.

969
00:40:18,550 --> 00:40:20,274
[Speaking German]

970
00:40:20,309 --> 00:40:22,309
-"You think this outfit
is too busy?"

971
00:40:25,998 --> 00:40:28,722
-We'd better start now.
It will be light soon.

972
00:40:28,757 --> 00:40:30,895
-Herr Klein.
-Ja.

973
00:40:30,929 --> 00:40:32,136
-I thought the locals
were afraid

974
00:40:32,171 --> 00:40:33,722
of going up that mountain.

975
00:40:33,757 --> 00:40:36,550
-They are afraid.
There's an unwritten law here.

976
00:40:36,584 --> 00:40:39,067
Time like this, they --
they go up, no matter what.

977
00:40:39,102 --> 00:40:40,274
-Even without pants.

978
00:40:40,309 --> 00:40:42,515
-I have called a spotter plane.

979
00:40:42,550 --> 00:40:44,895
But it will not be up
till morning.

980
00:40:44,929 --> 00:40:46,757
We will be well
on our way by then.

981
00:40:46,791 --> 00:40:48,446
Ready?
-All right, Herr Klein.

982
00:40:48,481 --> 00:40:49,688
-Good.
-"Should I have my glasses

983
00:40:49,722 --> 00:40:50,688
on or off for this climb?"

984
00:40:50,722 --> 00:40:52,515
-Look after yourself, Alan.

985
00:40:52,550 --> 00:40:53,653
-Don't worry.

986
00:40:53,688 --> 00:40:55,619
I'll stop by the observatory
on the way down.

987
00:40:55,653 --> 00:40:56,895
-Good.

988
00:40:56,929 --> 00:41:00,102
Be careful, everybody.

989
00:41:00,136 --> 00:41:01,998
-"Don't lose your heads."

990
00:41:02,033 --> 00:41:04,964
-"These four brave men --"

991
00:41:04,998 --> 00:41:06,412
-"Aah!"

992
00:41:06,446 --> 00:41:09,378
-"...three brave men --"
-"Aah!"

993
00:41:09,412 --> 00:41:13,964
-"Uh, two brave men, soon to go
down in the annals of history."

994
00:41:13,998 --> 00:41:15,102
-"Look at that staircase.

995
00:41:15,136 --> 00:41:16,378
Would you look
at the staircase?"

996
00:41:16,412 --> 00:41:17,584
-"Wow.
What a staircase."

997
00:41:17,619 --> 00:41:19,860
-"Man, what a staircase.
Boy, I'm getting winded.

998
00:41:19,895 --> 00:41:21,481
How about you, Forrest?"

999
00:41:21,515 --> 00:41:24,722
"Oh, yeah."
-"Hey, quit bunching up."

1000
00:41:24,757 --> 00:41:25,826
-"What's that?"

1001
00:41:25,860 --> 00:41:26,860
-"Got enough left
for this staircase."

1002
00:41:26,895 --> 00:41:29,274
-"Whoa."

1003
00:41:29,309 --> 00:41:31,929
-"Watch your step."

1004
00:41:31,964 --> 00:41:33,964
-Why did they bring
so much licorice?

1005
00:41:36,446 --> 00:41:37,584
[Groaning]

1006
00:41:37,619 --> 00:41:40,274
-Hey, she's chasing rabbits
in her sleep.

1007
00:41:40,309 --> 00:41:43,619
-Here they come.
-Professor.

1008
00:41:43,653 --> 00:41:46,619
-"Thumper, Fluffy, Bugs."

1009
00:41:46,653 --> 00:41:49,619
-"Rico, Youngblood."
-He's climbing the ledge.

1010
00:41:49,653 --> 00:41:51,688
He'll be here soon.

1011
00:41:51,722 --> 00:41:53,653
Remember the hut.

1012
00:41:53,688 --> 00:41:54,998
Keep him away.

1013
00:41:55,033 --> 00:41:57,136
Keep him away from the hut!

1014
00:41:57,171 --> 00:41:59,653
Away from the hut.
Away from the hut.

1015
00:41:59,688 --> 00:42:02,964
-And Starsky.
-Hut, hut, hut 1, hut 2.

1016
00:42:06,688 --> 00:42:08,136
Poor kooky kid.

1017
00:42:08,171 --> 00:42:10,860
-Maybe she shouldn't
sleep so much.

1018
00:42:10,895 --> 00:42:13,826
-Professor, what's the matter?

1019
00:42:13,860 --> 00:42:15,412
Why does she behave like this?

1020
00:42:15,446 --> 00:42:19,102
-She's a method actor.
-Well...

1021
00:42:19,136 --> 00:42:23,998
Well, it's like
a radio receiver.

1022
00:42:24,033 --> 00:42:28,515
You see, your sister's mind
is capable of receiving signals

1023
00:42:28,550 --> 00:42:30,378
sent out by other minds.

1024
00:42:30,412 --> 00:42:31,550
By yours, for example.

1025
00:42:31,584 --> 00:42:33,102
-"Let's start
with a simple test.

1026
00:42:33,136 --> 00:42:34,446
Can you tell where
I'm looking right now?"

1027
00:42:34,481 --> 00:42:36,274
-Now there is a stronger signal,I think.

1028
00:42:36,309 --> 00:42:38,619
A stronger mind.

1029
00:42:38,653 --> 00:42:40,274
It's jamming the wavelength.

1030
00:42:40,309 --> 00:42:43,791
-"Is she AM or FM?"
-But who is it?

1031
00:42:43,826 --> 00:42:45,550
-Well, we shall know more
about that, I think,

1032
00:42:45,584 --> 00:42:48,446
when they reach the hut.

1033
00:42:48,481 --> 00:42:50,446
In the meantime,
I say to you again,

1034
00:42:50,481 --> 00:42:53,722
get your sister out of here
before it is too late.

1035
00:42:53,757 --> 00:42:55,309
-Too late for what?

1036
00:42:55,343 --> 00:42:58,171
-"Last call? Take-out?
Too late for heroes?

1037
00:42:58,205 --> 00:43:01,067
Tell me!"

1038
00:43:01,102 --> 00:43:03,550
-Well, it's time I was getting
back to the observatory.

1039
00:43:03,584 --> 00:43:05,964
-Too late for what, Professor?
-"Last call?

1040
00:43:05,998 --> 00:43:07,653
Tell me!
You're keeping this from me."

1041
00:43:07,688 --> 00:43:10,136
-When they reach the hut,

1042
00:43:10,171 --> 00:43:11,757
then we
will know for sure, I think.

1043
00:43:11,791 --> 00:43:14,343
-"Ah."

1044
00:43:14,378 --> 00:43:17,929
[Knocking]

1045
00:43:17,964 --> 00:43:19,378
-The door's locked
from the inside.

1046
00:43:19,412 --> 00:43:21,412
They must be in there.

1047
00:43:21,446 --> 00:43:23,171
Mr. Brett!
Mr. Dewhurst!

1048
00:43:23,205 --> 00:43:24,136
-Had a look in
through the window.

1049
00:43:24,171 --> 00:43:25,791
The place seems deserted.

1050
00:43:26,171 --> 00:43:28,584
[Banging]

1051
00:43:38,274 --> 00:43:40,550
-"Hey, a doorknob.
Can I keep it?"

1052
00:43:40,584 --> 00:43:44,067
-Philip, come here.

1053
00:43:44,102 --> 00:43:47,033
-"Oh!
Ow."

1054
00:43:47,067 --> 00:43:49,964
-These blankets -- frozen stiff.

1055
00:43:49,998 --> 00:43:52,550
-"Must've slept with his hand
in lukewarm water."

1056
00:43:52,584 --> 00:43:54,481
-Where are they?

1057
00:43:54,515 --> 00:43:56,688
-That door was barred
on the inside.

1058
00:43:56,722 --> 00:43:58,378
-Yeah.
-Herr Brooks!

1059
00:44:02,067 --> 00:44:05,102
-Pants, with the leg
still in them.

1060
00:44:05,136 --> 00:44:07,033
-Those are the best kind.

1061
00:44:07,067 --> 00:44:09,309
-"Careful not to pull too hard.

1062
00:44:09,343 --> 00:44:12,102
His head might be caught
on something."

1063
00:44:12,136 --> 00:44:15,274
-"He's huge!"
-Dewhurst...

1064
00:44:17,274 --> 00:44:19,033
His head's been torn off.

1065
00:44:19,067 --> 00:44:20,274
-"Ripped off."

1066
00:44:20,309 --> 00:44:23,343
-Anne, we're leaving here today.

1067
00:44:23,378 --> 00:44:24,722
-No, Sarah.

1068
00:44:24,757 --> 00:44:26,722
Why not? Look, after last night,surely --

1069
00:44:26,757 --> 00:44:28,550
-I just want to stay here.
That's all.

1070
00:44:28,584 --> 00:44:29,826
-"We haven't tried
the fish yet."

1071
00:44:29,860 --> 00:44:31,412
-Now, Anne, I really mean this.

1072
00:44:31,446 --> 00:44:34,274
We're leaving Trollenberg today.

1073
00:44:34,309 --> 00:44:37,171
All right?
-"You're thinking of a color.

1074
00:44:39,653 --> 00:44:41,205
There's someone
in the room named Bob."

1075
00:44:41,240 --> 00:44:42,412
-Shut up.

1076
00:44:42,446 --> 00:44:45,171
-"Two superpowers will unite
to fight a third."

1077
00:44:45,205 --> 00:44:46,309
-Stop it!

1078
00:44:46,343 --> 00:44:47,722
-"Two brothers will reach
the Senate.

1079
00:44:47,757 --> 00:44:49,343
One will become President.
Both will die."

1080
00:44:49,378 --> 00:44:51,205
-Shh!
Hush.

1081
00:44:51,240 --> 00:45:00,826
♪♪

1082
00:45:00,860 --> 00:45:01,998
-"Can I have that toast?

1083
00:45:02,033 --> 00:45:03,929
Are you gonna eat that toast?"

1084
00:45:03,964 --> 00:45:06,895
♪♪

1085
00:45:06,929 --> 00:45:09,102
[Beeping]

1086
00:45:09,136 --> 00:45:12,964
-The matte painting
is calling you.

1087
00:45:12,998 --> 00:45:15,550
Go to the window.

1088
00:45:15,584 --> 00:45:18,171
[Beeping]

1089
00:45:18,205 --> 00:45:24,033
♪♪

1090
00:45:24,067 --> 00:45:26,136
-Go to the closet.

1091
00:45:26,171 --> 00:45:30,964
♪♪

1092
00:45:30,998 --> 00:45:34,826
Unpack your clothes.

1093
00:45:34,860 --> 00:45:36,033
Open your suitcase.

1094
00:45:36,067 --> 00:45:37,998
Take out a foundation garment.

1095
00:45:38,033 --> 00:45:39,964
Cut to another scene.

1096
00:45:39,998 --> 00:45:44,343
-Keep it hid.
-"You guys take the torso."

1097
00:45:44,378 --> 00:45:48,067
-Joel, carry me, will you?
-I'm getting outta here.

1098
00:45:48,102 --> 00:45:49,619
-Herr Brett is
an experienced climber,

1099
00:45:49,653 --> 00:45:52,412
so we can only assume
that he's hurt.

1100
00:45:52,446 --> 00:45:53,619
We all better search.

1101
00:45:53,653 --> 00:45:56,240
The little spotter plane
could be here any minute.

1102
00:45:57,929 --> 00:46:02,653
[Theme music playing]

1103
00:46:04,688 --> 00:46:07,446
-He's not gonna like this.

1104
00:46:07,481 --> 00:46:09,171
-And I'm not gonna tell him.

1105
00:46:09,205 --> 00:46:10,964
You tell him.

1106
00:46:10,998 --> 00:46:13,033
-Well, maybe he won't notice.

1107
00:46:13,067 --> 00:46:15,205
-Oh, yeah, right.

1108
00:46:15,240 --> 00:46:18,446
Hey, Cambot, could you speed
this up a little bit?

1109
00:46:18,481 --> 00:46:19,964
-Uh-oh.
-Oh.

1110
00:46:19,998 --> 00:46:21,412
He's here.
Oh.

1111
00:46:21,446 --> 00:46:24,550
-Uh, Joel, I don't to be
a teacher's pet or anything,

1112
00:46:24,584 --> 00:46:26,584
but Gypsy
uncoiled herself again!

1113
00:46:26,619 --> 00:46:27,998
-Yeah, she did.

1114
00:46:28,033 --> 00:46:30,722
Cambot, could you widen it out
so everybody can see this?

1115
00:46:30,757 --> 00:46:33,102
Gypsy, I know you're in here!

1116
00:46:33,136 --> 00:46:34,653
Quit hiding!

1117
00:46:34,688 --> 00:46:37,653
You clown, get out here!
Oh.

1118
00:46:37,688 --> 00:46:39,619
Oh, let's do that thing
we did, okay?

1119
00:46:39,653 --> 00:46:41,964
-All right.
-It's too bad Gypsy's not here,

1120
00:46:41,998 --> 00:46:43,102
huh, Crow and Servo?

1121
00:46:43,136 --> 00:46:44,515
-Yeah.
-It's too bad she's gonna

1122
00:46:44,550 --> 00:46:46,791
miss out
on those piping-hot RAM chips.

1123
00:46:46,826 --> 00:46:48,722
-Mm-hmm.
-RAM chips?

1124
00:46:48,757 --> 00:46:49,826
-Oh, all right.

1125
00:46:49,860 --> 00:46:51,309
I knew you were around here
somewhere.

1126
00:46:51,343 --> 00:46:55,653
Listen, you know you're not
supposed to come out

1127
00:46:55,688 --> 00:46:57,619
without using
the service portal.

1128
00:46:57,653 --> 00:46:59,136
Now, look,
I want to show you this.

1129
00:46:59,171 --> 00:47:01,102
See this big mess you got?
[Gypsy wailing]

1130
00:47:01,136 --> 00:47:02,895
All over the place.
That's --

1131
00:47:02,929 --> 00:47:04,929
-Put it back! Put it back!
-Hey, this is --

1132
00:47:04,964 --> 00:47:06,240
this is how you look, okay?

1133
00:47:06,274 --> 00:47:09,895
Now, otherwise, we're gonna haveexoservo spinal bundle

1134
00:47:09,929 --> 00:47:11,274
all over the place.

1135
00:47:11,309 --> 00:47:13,515
-What?
-The black stuff.

1136
00:47:13,550 --> 00:47:15,481
-Oh.
Hey, Joel?

1137
00:47:15,515 --> 00:47:17,412
-What's that?
-I got an itch.

1138
00:47:17,446 --> 00:47:18,722
-Oh, terrific.

1139
00:47:18,757 --> 00:47:20,481
She's got an itch.
Okay.

1140
00:47:20,515 --> 00:47:21,688
-Happy day.
-Okay.

1141
00:47:21,722 --> 00:47:23,274
Where is it, Gypsy?

1142
00:47:23,309 --> 00:47:26,619
-Vertebrae 6805 through 7219.

1143
00:47:26,653 --> 00:47:30,378
-Vertebrae 6905 through 7018.

1144
00:47:30,412 --> 00:47:32,102
Okay, you guys,
start scratching.

1145
00:47:32,136 --> 00:47:34,412
Where's my rake?
-Whoa!

1146
00:47:34,446 --> 00:47:37,240
-I hate it
when she gets diode rash.

1147
00:47:37,274 --> 00:47:39,550
-Me too.

1148
00:47:39,584 --> 00:47:41,343
Uh, Tom Servo, I think

1149
00:47:41,378 --> 00:47:44,136
you're scratching on
the solar collector cable.

1150
00:47:44,171 --> 00:47:45,412
-Oh, right.
-So am I.

1151
00:47:45,446 --> 00:47:46,722
Wait a minute now, here.

1152
00:47:46,757 --> 00:47:48,722
-Why am I doing this?
I hate being the end man.

1153
00:47:48,757 --> 00:47:50,550
I scratch her,
and it takes her six hours

1154
00:47:50,584 --> 00:47:52,309
to feel it in her head.

1155
00:47:52,343 --> 00:47:55,964
-Uh, Joel, uh, I don't want
to ask a stupid question,

1156
00:47:55,998 --> 00:47:58,309
but why'd you make her so big?

1157
00:47:58,343 --> 00:47:59,860
-Well, you know how --

1158
00:47:59,895 --> 00:48:03,171
It's kind of like when you startconnecting paper clips together.

1159
00:48:03,205 --> 00:48:04,481
You get hooked on it.

1160
00:48:04,515 --> 00:48:06,688
It's kind of like that.

1161
00:48:06,722 --> 00:48:08,136
-Oh, my.

1162
00:48:08,171 --> 00:48:09,515
-What's that, Tom?

1163
00:48:09,550 --> 00:48:11,826
-Joel, you remember when we
were speculating on her sex?

1164
00:48:11,860 --> 00:48:13,136
-Yeah.
-Well, I think I may

1165
00:48:13,171 --> 00:48:14,653
have narrowed it down here.

1166
00:48:14,688 --> 00:48:16,033
-Oh, yeah?
-Uh...

1167
00:48:16,067 --> 00:48:17,791
Oh. Oh. Check that.

1168
00:48:17,826 --> 00:48:19,067
Sorry.
False alarm.

1169
00:48:19,102 --> 00:48:21,688
-Here's the --
I think I found the problem.

1170
00:48:21,722 --> 00:48:23,722
She's jammed in here
behind the door.

1171
00:48:23,757 --> 00:48:26,929
-Hey, don't open
that door, McGee!

1172
00:48:26,964 --> 00:48:28,378
-Happens every time.

1173
00:48:28,412 --> 00:48:31,240
We got movie sign!

1174
00:48:31,274 --> 00:48:35,240
[Motors whirring]

1175
00:48:42,446 --> 00:48:44,722
-I feel safe in your arms, Joel.

1176
00:48:53,722 --> 00:48:56,067
-Plane to party.
Plane to party.

1177
00:48:56,102 --> 00:48:57,998
Are you receiving me?
-"Party here.

1178
00:48:58,033 --> 00:48:59,619
Pick up some ice
and some cubes."

1179
00:48:59,653 --> 00:49:01,964
-Party to plane --
receiving you loud and clear.

1180
00:49:01,998 --> 00:49:06,102
-"Get some paper cups."
-What is your position?

1181
00:49:06,136 --> 00:49:13,378
-Our position -- map reference,
265-843, over.

1182
00:49:13,412 --> 00:49:14,619
-"Can't miss it.

1183
00:49:14,653 --> 00:49:17,964
Dave's van's parked
right out in front."

1184
00:49:17,998 --> 00:49:19,929
[Engine droning]

1185
00:49:19,964 --> 00:49:22,205
-Vroom!
-Roger, party.

1186
00:49:22,240 --> 00:49:23,964
I can see you now.

1187
00:49:23,998 --> 00:49:26,550
I shall fly north for 5 miles...

1188
00:49:26,584 --> 00:49:28,550
-For no apparent reason."
-...then further west

1189
00:49:28,584 --> 00:49:29,757
for 5 miles,
then south.

1190
00:49:29,791 --> 00:49:31,136
-Uh-huh.

1191
00:49:31,171 --> 00:49:32,895
-Then you'll be back
in the exact same spot.

1192
00:49:32,929 --> 00:49:34,722
-Roger, plane.

1193
00:49:34,757 --> 00:49:36,964
[Airplane engine droning]

1194
00:49:39,033 --> 00:49:40,067
-I --

1195
00:49:40,102 --> 00:49:41,895
-"What the...?"

1196
00:49:41,929 --> 00:49:45,446
-She didn't make her bed again.
-She's...

1197
00:49:45,481 --> 00:49:46,446
This is her nightgown.

1198
00:49:46,481 --> 00:49:48,033
-She's not in there.

1199
00:49:48,067 --> 00:49:50,860
-"She's been digging
through my stuff."

1200
00:49:50,895 --> 00:49:53,964
-"Let's see.
She went to the closet.

1201
00:49:53,998 --> 00:49:56,791
Hey, she took my stuff.

1202
00:49:56,826 --> 00:49:58,653
Then she must've gone
to the window."

1203
00:49:58,688 --> 00:50:03,791
♪♪

1204
00:50:03,826 --> 00:50:05,171
-I didn't know they had cable.

1205
00:50:05,205 --> 00:50:11,240
-♪ The hills are alive,
and it's getting scary ♪

1206
00:50:11,274 --> 00:50:14,964
♪ That cloud over there
wasn't there before ♪

1207
00:50:18,067 --> 00:50:19,033
-Hello?

1208
00:50:19,067 --> 00:50:20,067
-Hello.

1209
00:50:20,102 --> 00:50:21,102
-Hello?
-Hello.

1210
00:50:21,136 --> 00:50:23,102
-I want to speak
with the observatory.

1211
00:50:23,136 --> 00:50:24,757
Professor Crevett.

1212
00:50:27,446 --> 00:50:29,481
-"De plane!
De plane!"

1213
00:50:33,446 --> 00:50:36,998
-"Don't crash, don't crash,
don't crash.

1214
00:50:37,033 --> 00:50:41,205
If I concentrate,
I won't crash."

1215
00:50:41,240 --> 00:50:44,102
-"Let's see.
Let's find a flat spot here."

1216
00:50:44,136 --> 00:50:46,481
-"Whoa!
Here's the party!"

1217
00:50:48,757 --> 00:50:50,619
-Hello, party.
This is the plane.

1218
00:50:50,653 --> 00:50:52,550
I've spotted him.
I've spotted him.

1219
00:50:52,584 --> 00:50:56,412
He's approximately half a mile
due north of your position,

1220
00:50:56,446 --> 00:50:58,274
above the north face.

1221
00:50:58,309 --> 00:51:01,550
-Roger, plane.

1222
00:51:01,584 --> 00:51:03,378
-Big part for him.

1223
00:51:08,481 --> 00:51:09,791
-Look out for the mountain!

1224
00:51:09,826 --> 00:51:11,860
[Imitates explosion]

1225
00:51:11,895 --> 00:51:16,171
♪♪

1226
00:51:16,205 --> 00:51:18,102
-"Hi. I'm the curator
of the matte painting."

1227
00:51:18,136 --> 00:51:20,309
-Miss Pilgrim, my name's Wilde.

1228
00:51:20,343 --> 00:51:23,274
Professor Crevett sent meto bring you to the observatory.

1229
00:51:23,309 --> 00:51:24,860
-But I'm not to go
to the observatory.

1230
00:51:24,895 --> 00:51:26,067
-Well, there's nowhere else
to go out here.

1231
00:51:26,102 --> 00:51:28,791
And it's only
a couple hundred yards.

1232
00:51:28,826 --> 00:51:31,412
-I'm to go up the mountain.

1233
00:51:31,446 --> 00:51:33,929
-I don't think so, Miss Pilgrim.

1234
00:51:33,964 --> 00:51:37,343
I think you ought to wait
a while at the observatory.

1235
00:51:37,378 --> 00:51:41,446
-"What color is the sky
in your world?"

1236
00:51:41,481 --> 00:51:43,929
-"Just about.
Almost, almost."

1237
00:51:43,964 --> 00:51:46,205
[Grunting]

1238
00:51:46,240 --> 00:51:48,964
[Speaking German]

1239
00:51:48,998 --> 00:51:50,412
-"What?!"

1240
00:51:50,446 --> 00:51:52,412
-He said, "Here's a bloody bag."

1241
00:51:52,446 --> 00:51:57,964
♪♪

1242
00:51:57,998 --> 00:52:03,584
♪♪

1243
00:52:03,619 --> 00:52:05,309
Better have a look-see.

1244
00:52:05,343 --> 00:52:07,515
[Echoing]
-Herr Brett!

1245
00:52:07,550 --> 00:52:10,964
-Echo!

1246
00:52:10,998 --> 00:52:12,653
-"Need to shave."

1247
00:52:14,757 --> 00:52:17,378
-"Oh.

1248
00:52:17,412 --> 00:52:18,378
Pudding?"

1249
00:52:18,412 --> 00:52:19,722
-Maybe it's somebody's lunch.

1250
00:52:19,757 --> 00:52:22,550
-Hey, kommen sie hier!
Schnell!

1251
00:52:22,584 --> 00:52:24,998
-"Kommen sie hier, schnell"?

1252
00:52:25,033 --> 00:52:27,205
-"Alright?
S'alright."

1253
00:52:27,240 --> 00:52:31,550
-"We found Hoffman."
"You looked in my bag!"

1254
00:52:31,584 --> 00:52:35,895
-Hey, buddy, it won't get betterif you pick at it.

1255
00:52:35,929 --> 00:52:38,343
-"Uh, yeah, your friend, I got
a bone to pick with you, too."

1256
00:52:38,378 --> 00:52:40,584
-I suppose you want to look
in my bag, too!"

1257
00:52:40,619 --> 00:52:42,412
-"And it's yours."

1258
00:52:42,446 --> 00:52:44,033
That's gotta hurt.

1259
00:52:44,067 --> 00:52:45,309
-Thirty degrees west.

1260
00:52:45,343 --> 00:52:49,446
[Whirring]

1261
00:52:49,481 --> 00:52:53,412
How about that?
-Negative.

1262
00:52:53,446 --> 00:52:55,584
-Hubert?
-Negative.

1263
00:52:59,136 --> 00:53:00,791
-Swing another 30 degrees.

1264
00:53:00,826 --> 00:53:04,343
[Whirring]

1265
00:53:04,378 --> 00:53:06,033
-Negative.
-Everybody's so negative.

1266
00:53:06,067 --> 00:53:07,929
-Negative.

1267
00:53:10,309 --> 00:53:13,791
-Try the other circuit.

1268
00:53:13,826 --> 00:53:15,274
Oh, wait.

1269
00:53:15,309 --> 00:53:18,067
Switch back to the first circuit
and reverse this time.

1270
00:53:20,860 --> 00:53:21,791
Now.

1271
00:53:21,826 --> 00:53:23,929
-This will never replace
Nintendo.

1272
00:53:23,964 --> 00:53:25,550
-"Now's my chance to beat it."

1273
00:53:25,584 --> 00:53:28,895
-"Hope I don't run into
Forrest Tucker.

1274
00:53:28,929 --> 00:53:30,791
Oh!"
-Oh, hello, Anne.

1275
00:53:30,826 --> 00:53:33,515
What are you doing up here?

1276
00:53:33,550 --> 00:53:34,619
-"Uh, nothing."

1277
00:53:34,653 --> 00:53:36,446
-Probably waiting
for the commercial.

1278
00:53:36,481 --> 00:53:38,929
-Go?
Go where?

1279
00:53:38,964 --> 00:53:41,240
-The Trollenberg --
I must go now.

1280
00:53:41,274 --> 00:53:42,550
-Don't let her go.

1281
00:53:42,584 --> 00:53:44,136
-Don't worry.

1282
00:53:44,171 --> 00:53:45,515
Come along, Anne.

1283
00:53:51,826 --> 00:53:54,240
-Oh, good.
Alan, I want to speak with you.

1284
00:53:54,274 --> 00:53:56,550
-Yeah, I got news for you, too.

1285
00:53:56,584 --> 00:53:59,274
Hans, when's the next cable car
go down the mountain?

1286
00:53:59,309 --> 00:54:01,067
-It shouldn't be long, sir.
-Good, I --

1287
00:54:01,102 --> 00:54:02,791
I want you to take Anne down.

1288
00:54:02,826 --> 00:54:04,998
Don't let her out of your sight.

1289
00:54:05,033 --> 00:54:07,653
Anne, I want you
to go with Hans.

1290
00:54:07,688 --> 00:54:09,102
-"He's a warm and wonderful man.

1291
00:54:09,136 --> 00:54:12,584
You'll be very happy."
-Roping Hans.

1292
00:54:14,826 --> 00:54:17,515
-"Hey, everybody, I got a girl."

1293
00:54:17,550 --> 00:54:19,584
"They make a lovely couple,
don't they?"

1294
00:54:24,309 --> 00:54:26,274
-Well, I've been to the hut.

1295
00:54:26,309 --> 00:54:27,550
-And?

1296
00:54:27,584 --> 00:54:29,412
-Dewhurst is dead.

1297
00:54:29,446 --> 00:54:31,102
Brett's missing.

1298
00:54:31,136 --> 00:54:33,826
-[Sighs]

1299
00:54:33,860 --> 00:54:37,102
Not that I'm surprised,
mind you.

1300
00:54:37,136 --> 00:54:40,378
The cloud has
started moving again.

1301
00:54:40,412 --> 00:54:41,619
-Ah.
-What cloud.

1302
00:54:41,653 --> 00:54:42,895
-Bozo the cloud.

1303
00:54:42,929 --> 00:54:45,378
-I don't think you'd understand,Philip.

1304
00:54:45,412 --> 00:54:46,998
-"You're much too stupid."

1305
00:54:47,033 --> 00:54:49,378
-Like that time in the Andes,
you mean?

1306
00:54:49,412 --> 00:54:51,860
-"Hmm?"
-How do you know about that?

1307
00:54:51,895 --> 00:54:53,998
-"I get around."
-All right.

1308
00:54:54,033 --> 00:54:55,205
I'm a newspaperman.

1309
00:54:55,240 --> 00:54:56,240
It's my job to know
about these things.

1310
00:54:56,274 --> 00:54:58,136
-"Oh!"

1311
00:54:58,171 --> 00:55:00,515
-I see.

1312
00:55:00,550 --> 00:55:04,309
-I chased that story when we gotwind of your report, Alan.

1313
00:55:04,343 --> 00:55:06,791
When I got there,
it was too late.

1314
00:55:06,826 --> 00:55:09,688
Look as if I'm gonna be more
fortunate this time, isn't it?

1315
00:55:09,722 --> 00:55:10,895
-Yeah, fine, fine.

1316
00:55:10,929 --> 00:55:13,378
That's --
That's just what we need.

1317
00:55:15,688 --> 00:55:19,412
-Haven't answered my question,
Professor.

1318
00:55:19,446 --> 00:55:20,688
-What question?

1319
00:55:20,722 --> 00:55:22,964
-Is this the same
as it was then?

1320
00:55:22,998 --> 00:55:25,412
-"Well, that was then,
and this is now."

1321
00:55:25,446 --> 00:55:28,033
-So, what happens now?
-"This."

1322
00:55:28,067 --> 00:55:29,964
-The United Nations had
a special team

1323
00:55:29,998 --> 00:55:31,964
investigating phenomena.

1324
00:55:31,998 --> 00:55:33,653
I call them in.

1325
00:55:33,688 --> 00:55:35,791
-"They also double as
our softball team."

1326
00:55:35,826 --> 00:55:37,688
-Does the theory that you put
forward last time

1327
00:55:37,722 --> 00:55:38,964
still hold, Professor?

1328
00:55:38,998 --> 00:55:41,929
-What theory?
-Visitors from outer space.

1329
00:55:41,964 --> 00:55:43,171
That was it, wasn't it?

1330
00:55:43,205 --> 00:55:45,171
-And what else?
-Well, what are they?

1331
00:55:45,205 --> 00:55:46,860
What do they want?

1332
00:55:46,895 --> 00:55:48,584
-Look, Philip...
-The fish.

1333
00:55:48,619 --> 00:55:50,998
-...there are many galaxies
besides ours.

1334
00:55:51,033 --> 00:55:54,860
Now, who knows what is happeningmillions of miles out in space?

1335
00:55:54,895 --> 00:55:56,378
-Sagan knows.
-Perhaps the world

1336
00:55:56,412 --> 00:55:59,584
that these creatures
inhabit is coming to an end.

1337
00:55:59,619 --> 00:56:01,929
Perhaps they need to find
somewhere else to live.

1338
00:56:01,964 --> 00:56:05,584
-Well, why pick on us?
-We don't know it's just us.

1339
00:56:05,619 --> 00:56:07,722
Maybe it's also Mars, Venus,
Jupiter.

1340
00:56:07,757 --> 00:56:10,722
Who can tell?

1341
00:56:10,757 --> 00:56:12,378
-Do you go along with this?

1342
00:56:12,412 --> 00:56:15,481
-Well, until somebody comes up
with a better theory...

1343
00:56:15,515 --> 00:56:18,067
-Or a sitcom.
-...yes, yes, I do.

1344
00:56:18,102 --> 00:56:20,481
-All right.
All right.

1345
00:56:20,515 --> 00:56:24,515
While we're theorizing, why doyou think, do they always happen

1346
00:56:24,550 --> 00:56:25,998
to land
on the top of a mountain?

1347
00:56:26,033 --> 00:56:28,791
First, the Andes,
and now the Trollenberg.

1348
00:56:28,826 --> 00:56:30,895
-Well, it's the atmosphere,
I think.

1349
00:56:30,929 --> 00:56:34,378
You see, it gets
much thinner high up.

1350
00:56:34,412 --> 00:56:36,998
Perhaps these
creatures need that.

1351
00:56:37,033 --> 00:56:39,584
The cloud would seem
to indicate this, too

1352
00:56:39,619 --> 00:56:41,688
Creating their own atmosphere.

1353
00:56:41,722 --> 00:56:43,584
-Well, if they can only exist
on the tops of mountains,

1354
00:56:43,619 --> 00:56:46,171
they're hardly likely to inheritthe Earth, are they?

1355
00:56:46,205 --> 00:56:49,826
-You see, anyone can get used
to anything in time.

1356
00:56:49,860 --> 00:56:51,033
-"Like I you."

1357
00:56:51,067 --> 00:56:53,481
-Now, these movements we have
recorded here,

1358
00:56:53,515 --> 00:56:56,550
each time,
lower down the mountain.

1359
00:56:56,584 --> 00:56:58,757
The climitization effect.

1360
00:56:58,791 --> 00:57:00,309
-What next, Professor?

1361
00:57:00,343 --> 00:57:02,929
-The next move
is up to you, Alan.

1362
00:57:02,964 --> 00:57:06,240
You must inform the authorities.

1363
00:57:06,274 --> 00:57:07,550
-Or the authorities.

1364
00:57:07,584 --> 00:57:09,412
-Have you seen the professor?

1365
00:57:09,446 --> 00:57:10,653
-He's in the office
with Alan Brooks.

1366
00:57:10,688 --> 00:57:13,550
They're telephoning Zurich.

1367
00:57:13,584 --> 00:57:15,033
-Any sign of Mr. Brett yet?

1368
00:57:15,067 --> 00:57:17,309
-Well, the plane spotted
someone he thought was him,

1369
00:57:17,343 --> 00:57:19,964
and two of the searchers
went to meet him.

1370
00:57:19,998 --> 00:57:22,826
The others are back.
They saw nothing.

1371
00:57:22,860 --> 00:57:25,481
-Well, if it was Brett,
they ought to be back by now.

1372
00:57:28,171 --> 00:57:30,550
-Where's your sister?
-She's in her room.

1373
00:57:30,584 --> 00:57:31,998
-Good.

1374
00:57:32,033 --> 00:57:35,309
-You think she's safe
from our friends up there?

1375
00:57:35,343 --> 00:57:38,067
-Well, I don't think they'll
try the same thing again.

1376
00:57:38,102 --> 00:57:41,205
Trouble is, what will they try?

1377
00:57:43,378 --> 00:57:47,550
[Imitating theme from "The Good,the Bad and the Ugly"]

1378
00:57:47,584 --> 00:57:48,688
-You all right?!
-Wow.

1379
00:57:48,722 --> 00:57:52,550
He looks dead on his feet.
-A bit tired.

1380
00:57:52,584 --> 00:57:54,067
Otherwise, all right.

1381
00:57:54,102 --> 00:57:55,309
-Where have you been?!

1382
00:57:55,343 --> 00:57:57,274
We've had search parties
out for you!

1383
00:57:57,309 --> 00:57:59,446
-I was lost.

1384
00:57:59,481 --> 00:58:01,757
-In a fog, if you will.

1385
00:58:01,791 --> 00:58:05,722
-When did you see him last?
-Dewhurst?

1386
00:58:05,757 --> 00:58:08,274
-Yes.

1387
00:58:08,309 --> 00:58:10,343
-Well, last night I saw him.

1388
00:58:10,378 --> 00:58:11,412
-At the hut.

1389
00:58:11,446 --> 00:58:14,860
-Yes, at the hut.

1390
00:58:20,584 --> 00:58:23,446
It's a bit hot in here.

1391
00:58:23,481 --> 00:58:25,136
Pour me a drink, Klein.

1392
00:58:30,067 --> 00:58:31,688
-Have one on me.

1393
00:58:38,446 --> 00:58:40,136
-Let me help you.

1394
00:58:40,171 --> 00:58:41,722
-Sorry.

1395
00:58:43,619 --> 00:58:46,412
Thank you.

1396
00:58:46,446 --> 00:58:48,240
-He can't handle his liquor.

1397
00:58:53,446 --> 00:58:55,791
-How's your sister,
Miss Pilgrim?

1398
00:58:55,826 --> 00:58:57,412
-She's all right, now.

1399
00:58:57,446 --> 00:58:59,378
-Where is she?

1400
00:58:59,412 --> 00:59:01,550
-She's in --
-Here.

1401
00:59:01,584 --> 00:59:03,619
Have a cigarette.
-Great.

1402
00:59:03,653 --> 00:59:05,274
We saw how well he did
with the booze.

1403
00:59:05,309 --> 00:59:07,343
Now let's give him
something that's lit.

1404
00:59:15,826 --> 00:59:18,033
-[Imitating Raymond Babbitt]
"429 matches exactly.

1405
00:59:18,067 --> 00:59:20,481
Exactly 429."

1406
00:59:20,515 --> 00:59:22,171
-That's a good
impression, Servo.

1407
00:59:22,205 --> 00:59:25,067
-"I'm an excellent driver."

1408
00:59:25,102 --> 00:59:27,412
He's gonna light his nose!
Look out!

1409
00:59:27,446 --> 00:59:28,446
-What're they gonna have him
do next?

1410
00:59:28,481 --> 00:59:30,309
Have him drive
a forklift you guys?

1411
00:59:30,343 --> 00:59:33,860
Gee.
Glutton for punishing others.

1412
00:59:39,446 --> 00:59:42,860
-"Let's get the dog drunk next."

1413
00:59:42,895 --> 00:59:45,136
-It's too scary up there
on your own.

1414
00:59:45,171 --> 00:59:47,964
-Were you out on your own?

1415
00:59:47,998 --> 00:59:51,171
-After I lost Dewhurst.

1416
00:59:51,205 --> 00:59:53,205
-I'm sorry to be a nuisance,
but may I --

1417
00:59:53,240 --> 00:59:55,274
[Ominous music plays]

1418
00:59:55,309 --> 00:59:57,757
-Then she walked in.

1419
00:59:57,791 --> 01:00:01,688
♪♪

1420
01:00:01,722 --> 01:00:04,688
Ooh!
-He's kind of pigheaded.

1421
01:00:04,722 --> 01:00:07,240
-Oh, that smarts.

1422
01:00:07,274 --> 01:00:08,584
-Look at his head.

1423
01:00:08,619 --> 01:00:10,171
-At least he's got one.

1424
01:00:10,205 --> 01:00:11,964
-But...

1425
01:00:11,998 --> 01:00:14,412
there's no blood.

1426
01:00:14,446 --> 01:00:18,136
-"He's wearing
Mr Spock's jammies."

1427
01:00:18,171 --> 01:00:19,998
-Dig a little deeper.

1428
01:00:20,033 --> 01:00:24,136
-Yeah, that's a good idea --
solder him back together.

1429
01:00:24,171 --> 01:00:25,481
-There.

1430
01:00:25,515 --> 01:00:28,033
That should take care of him
till the morning, I think.

1431
01:00:28,067 --> 01:00:29,102
-Good.
-He should be up

1432
01:00:29,136 --> 01:00:30,688
and spilling liquor in no time.

1433
01:00:30,722 --> 01:00:33,895
-Dr. Becker can have
a look at him.

1434
01:00:33,929 --> 01:00:37,067
-Do we tell the others
what he was trying to do?

1435
01:00:39,757 --> 01:00:41,240
-I don't know.

1436
01:00:41,274 --> 01:00:42,860
-Hmm.

1437
01:00:46,205 --> 01:00:47,550
Looks great.

1438
01:00:50,998 --> 01:00:52,378
-"Let's lock him in here."
-I don't know about you,

1439
01:00:52,412 --> 01:00:53,378
but I could use a drink.

1440
01:00:53,412 --> 01:00:55,033
-So what else is new?

1441
01:00:57,895 --> 01:00:59,895
I think we all
could use a drink.

1442
01:00:59,929 --> 01:01:05,136
[Theme music playing]

1443
01:01:07,343 --> 01:01:09,446
♪♪

1444
01:01:09,481 --> 01:01:11,653
-"Who put this zipper
in my head?

1445
01:01:11,688 --> 01:01:18,619
♪♪

1446
01:01:18,653 --> 01:01:20,067
Wow.
What a party.

1447
01:01:20,102 --> 01:01:21,722
I'm dead tired.

1448
01:01:26,964 --> 01:01:28,343
Little thirsty, though.

1449
01:01:28,378 --> 01:01:31,550
I need a drink.
Maybe some formaldehyde.

1450
01:01:31,584 --> 01:01:33,309
Hope they serve the dead here."

1451
01:01:33,343 --> 01:01:40,619
♪♪

1452
01:01:40,653 --> 01:01:47,998
♪♪

1453
01:01:48,033 --> 01:01:50,412
-Looks like Popeye.
-[Mimics Popeye's laughter]

1454
01:01:50,446 --> 01:01:51,998
"Oh.

1455
01:01:52,033 --> 01:01:53,964
Somebody's comin'
down the hallways."

1456
01:01:57,136 --> 01:02:00,412
-"Huh. Doesn't look like
anybody's in there.

1457
01:02:00,446 --> 01:02:01,964
Well, I'll see about that.

1458
01:02:01,998 --> 01:02:03,584
Huh."

1459
01:02:03,619 --> 01:02:05,584
-"Yeah, I think I'll reach
through and get hims."

1460
01:02:05,619 --> 01:02:07,515
-"Hey, Popeye!
Ooh!"

1461
01:02:07,550 --> 01:02:10,826
♪ I'm Popeye the sailor man

1462
01:02:10,860 --> 01:02:13,584
♪ I've got a guy's head
in my hand ♪

1463
01:02:16,171 --> 01:02:19,067
-That's a legal submission hold.

1464
01:02:19,102 --> 01:02:21,136
-Sounds like a bad Popeye
impression to me.

1465
01:02:21,171 --> 01:02:26,584
♪♪

1466
01:02:26,619 --> 01:02:30,550
-I think you've
proved your point.

1467
01:02:30,584 --> 01:02:32,171
-Dropped him like a bad habit.

1468
01:02:32,205 --> 01:02:35,929
-He's just misunderstood.

1469
01:02:35,964 --> 01:02:38,757
-Most dead people are.

1470
01:02:38,791 --> 01:02:45,102
♪♪

1471
01:02:45,136 --> 01:02:47,412
-He'll make an excellent puppet.

1472
01:02:47,446 --> 01:02:50,102
There's Ward Cleaver.

1473
01:02:50,136 --> 01:02:52,205
-What do you think
he's gonna do with that?

1474
01:02:52,240 --> 01:02:59,412
♪♪

1475
01:02:59,446 --> 01:03:06,619
♪♪

1476
01:03:06,653 --> 01:03:13,791
♪♪

1477
01:03:13,826 --> 01:03:16,998
"Mind if I cut in?"

1478
01:03:17,033 --> 01:03:18,584
-"Here's Johnny."

1479
01:03:18,619 --> 01:03:21,205
-"Wendy, I'm home."

1480
01:03:21,240 --> 01:03:27,722
♪♪

1481
01:03:27,757 --> 01:03:34,240
♪♪

1482
01:03:34,274 --> 01:03:37,929
-"Pretty lady!"

1483
01:03:37,964 --> 01:03:41,343
"Norman, I told you not
to bother the guests."

1484
01:03:41,378 --> 01:03:43,550
-Alan! Alan!
Oh, Alan!

1485
01:03:43,584 --> 01:03:46,446
-Oh, my.
-Alan!

1486
01:03:46,481 --> 01:03:49,481
Alan, come quickly!

1487
01:03:49,515 --> 01:03:50,826
Alan, please!

1488
01:03:50,860 --> 01:03:52,619
Alan!

1489
01:03:52,653 --> 01:03:54,653
Alan, come quickly!

1490
01:03:54,688 --> 01:03:57,584
Alan!

1491
01:03:57,619 --> 01:03:59,653
-Aah!
[Gunshot]

1492
01:03:59,688 --> 01:04:02,481
-"And that's for killing
the fat guy."

1493
01:04:02,515 --> 01:04:06,378
-Whoa, carries a pistol
in his pajamas.

1494
01:04:06,412 --> 01:04:09,102
-What do you think
that pocket's for?

1495
01:04:09,136 --> 01:04:10,033
Kleenex?

1496
01:04:10,067 --> 01:04:12,240
-Well, look at this.
-What?

1497
01:04:12,274 --> 01:04:14,446
-Hans, bring the light closer.

1498
01:04:14,481 --> 01:04:18,343
-"Lower, lower, lower."
-Put that on the floor.

1499
01:04:18,378 --> 01:04:19,584
-"Even lower."

1500
01:04:19,619 --> 01:04:23,343
-See the way his flesh
reflects the light?

1501
01:04:23,378 --> 01:04:24,412
-"Pretty."

1502
01:04:24,446 --> 01:04:25,860
-Almost as though
it was crystallized.

1503
01:04:28,136 --> 01:04:30,515
Like meat
that's been deep frozen.

1504
01:04:30,550 --> 01:04:32,067
-I still don't understand
how such --

1505
01:04:32,102 --> 01:04:33,791
-Aah!
-Wow.

1506
01:04:33,826 --> 01:04:35,481
Unfortunate skin.

1507
01:04:38,412 --> 01:04:40,378
-Did we have to see that?
-Alan!

1508
01:04:40,412 --> 01:04:41,860
The observatory's just been on.

1509
01:04:41,895 --> 01:04:43,274
That cloud's moving again.

1510
01:04:43,309 --> 01:04:44,619
-Well, we haven't seen
the monster yet.

1511
01:04:44,653 --> 01:04:47,860
It's good to see something.

1512
01:04:47,895 --> 01:04:49,309
-It's coming.
-You're wasting your time.

1513
01:04:49,343 --> 01:04:51,481
That phone is dead.
The wires have been cut.

1514
01:04:51,515 --> 01:04:53,067
We've got to get up
to the observatory.

1515
01:04:53,102 --> 01:04:54,136
-But we must --

1516
01:04:54,171 --> 01:04:55,067
-You heard what
the professor said,

1517
01:04:55,102 --> 01:04:56,446
That place is built like a fort.

1518
01:04:56,481 --> 01:04:58,274
If we're gonna have any direct
contact with these things,

1519
01:04:58,309 --> 01:05:00,274
at least we'll have
some kind of a chance up there.

1520
01:05:00,309 --> 01:05:02,378
-It's insane!
We should try to get out!

1521
01:05:02,412 --> 01:05:03,826
To the valley!
-The road is blocked!

1522
01:05:03,860 --> 01:05:06,515
The cloud stretches
right across.

1523
01:05:06,550 --> 01:05:08,067
-"Now go out and do
something irrational."

1524
01:05:08,102 --> 01:05:09,274
-Yes, yes, you --

1525
01:05:09,309 --> 01:05:12,067
you go on the next cable car
with the girls.

1526
01:05:12,102 --> 01:05:14,343
-"Hey, dudes."
-You've got everyone?

1527
01:05:14,378 --> 01:05:16,240
Everyone.
Have 12 left.

1528
01:05:16,274 --> 01:05:17,481
One car's already gone up,

1529
01:05:17,515 --> 01:05:19,722
and the other one
will be down in 10 minutes.

1530
01:05:20,481 --> 01:05:22,550
[Engine revving]

1531
01:05:22,584 --> 01:05:23,826
-Who is that?
-"Hey."

1532
01:05:23,860 --> 01:05:27,205
-Hans!

1533
01:05:27,240 --> 01:05:29,309
"Hey, Hans, get some ice!"

1534
01:05:35,274 --> 01:05:36,964
-"Poor fool."

1535
01:05:36,998 --> 01:05:38,309
-All right, everybody,

1536
01:05:38,343 --> 01:05:39,860
let's get up to the cable car
as quickly as we can.

1537
01:05:39,895 --> 01:05:41,688
We haven't got very much time.
-"Oh, really?

1538
01:05:41,722 --> 01:05:44,378
I thought we'd just hang around
for an hour or two."

1539
01:05:48,446 --> 01:05:50,964
-You got everything?
-Uh-oh.

1540
01:05:50,998 --> 01:05:53,274
-What about Anne?-I bet they'll be back for that.

1541
01:05:53,309 --> 01:05:56,515
-...the cloud.

1542
01:05:56,550 --> 01:05:58,481
-I didn't know they got cable.

1543
01:05:58,515 --> 01:06:00,550
-Both channels.

1544
01:06:00,584 --> 01:06:05,481
♪♪

1545
